{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Nov 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384156800, 'question': 'Are they removable, If I need to get behind the furniture to clean?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes! Each side attaches to something (furniture and wall) but then there\'s a metal fitting/buckle and a "fabric" loop so you slide the metal buckle out of the loop to detach the furniture from the wall temporarily.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Feb 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392192000, 'question': 'Are these straps durable? I see the plastic on the ends and worry that might snap under a significant enough force.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'They go so tight that only a significant force could get enough force to pull it apart. We use it for our tv and two tall narrow bookcases without problems.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Jul 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404716400, 'question': 'how small are the fastners', 'answer': 'The straps themselves are about 1" wide and 17" long. The brackets that attach to the wall and furniture are about 1.5" wide and about 2.5" long. Once you attach each bracket the nylon straps cinch up tight. The length of the strap you\'ll need to reach behind to hook everything up.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Oct 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381734000, 'question': 'Do these work with furniture with a particle board backing?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'No. You need to attach it to a part of the furniture that the particle board is attached/nailed. The backing alone will not sustain the screws for the straps. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Jun 13, 2013', 'unixTime': 1371106800, 'question': 'Do the instructions recommend two straps per piece of furniture, or can smaller ones be secured with one?', 'answer': 'Instructions assume two straps for all furniture, no option for only one strap. I have a small night stand for my child and felt it could use one strap only, but did both straps as instructed. Probably overkill for such a small nightstand, but safer overall. Still a bit of a pain installing these things but worth the price.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Jan 5, 2009', 'unixTime': 1231142400, 'question': 'Securing furniture to a wall: Does anyone know of any type of securing device that could be used in a basement with cement walls?', 'answer': 'Try this link. Quakehold makes furniture bracing straps of various lengths. If you don\'t need 18", they also make a 15" strap. http://www.amazon.com/Quakehold-4160-18-Inch-Furniture-Strap/dp/B000FJRYAA/ref=sr_1_4?ie=UTF8&s=hi&qid=1231215404&sr=8-4'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Jan 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1390204800, 'question': 'Will these be sufficient in the event of an earthquake?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'We live in CA so that was a concern for us as well! We screwed these into the STUD (use a stud finder) and tested them by trying to get the dresser to tip with the weight of an adult. They work great! The person who had the screws snap must have done something very wrong, they said they used them on a shelf which is weird, these are for safety strapping furnature in case of accidental tipping (or a child climbing up) not to support a shelf.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Oct 16, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381906800, 'question': "Do these straps need to be secured to a stud? We have rows of bookcases and won't be able to line up each one with a stud.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Unless your bookcases are very narrow or you live in a non-standard house there should be a stud every 18 inches or so. I used the stud finder and marked where the studs were with a sticky note and then attached the strap where the stud was, as close to the middle of the bookcase as possible. The stud may not be in the center of the bookcase but that's ok- the strap will keep the bookcase from falling even if the strap is not exacly center..."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000056W7R', 'answerTime': 'Dec 27, 2013', 'unixTime': 1388131200, 'question': 'What is the maximum length of the straps fully extended when attached to furniture and wall?', 'answer': "We no longer have the straps, so I couldn't give you an exact measurement. If I had to guess I would say around 5 or 6 inches"}
