{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00073HJG8', 'answerTime': 'Aug 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407222000, 'question': 'Do you actually need to drill some wholes in the walls in order to hang this hammock?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It depends on your particular situation. You may be able to use 3M hooks that require no drilling or damage to the walls. IF this is a problem for you I would definitely recommend giving the 3M adhesive hooks (the ones with the pull tab for removing) a shot first. Otherwise, use screw in hooks to hit the wall stud and/or drywall anchors such as the tornado anchors that grip really secure. Either of these will, unfortunately, put holes in your wall. It can be repaired pretty easily if say you're renting and have to move out and make sure everything is fixed back for the landlord by using either a calk or drywall mud and putty knife and some carefully tinted paint to touch it up. Didn't mean to get technical but wanted to help you out as much as I could. Good luck."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00073HJG8', 'answerTime': 'Apr 9, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397026800, 'question': 'Do you know how far it sags? My only available corners are above a closet door or above windows.', 'answer': 'I hung it probably 8 inches in total from the cieling and the sag with stuffed animals in it is about 2 and a half feet from where the hooks are in the wall. Mind you this is only with a couple stuffed animals in it. I believe it stated the weight limit was 10 pounds and this is maybe 3 to 4 pounds max at this point.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00073HJG8', 'answerTime': 'Jul 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404975600, 'question': 'How many stuffed animals (medium and small) do you think it could hold?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Ours held about 20 stuff animals. That includes medium, small and a couple large ones. I highly recommend it.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00073HJG8', 'answerTime': 'Oct 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412924400, 'question': 'How to hang', 'answer': 'Determine where you want the toy hammock. Measure the same distance from the ceiling on each wall several feet from the corner. If the hammock has three hooks, measure down from the ceiling to a point in the corner also. Mark the locations with a pencil. Step 2 Double-check the location. Hold the hammock up on each end to make sure the holes are the correct distance apart. If the holes are too far apart, the hammock will not sag enough to hold the items. If the holes are too close, the hammock may sag too far down and touch furniture. Step 3 Drill pilot holes into your marked spots. Be sure to check the size of the mounting hooks to match the size of your drilled holes. Step 4 Twist in the mounting hooks. Make sure the hooks face upward and feel secure.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00073HJG8', 'answerTime': 'Jul 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405407600, 'question': "So corner anchor is in the ceiling. The other 2? The pic looks like they are in the ceiling, too, but many mentioned dry wall. What's best?", 'answer': "The anchors are placed in the ceiling. But they can also be placed in the wall; a bit awkward, but it works just as well. Depends if you're looking for studs to attach to and the type of wall/ceiling where they will be placed. But the choice is yours."}
