{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0007KMUH4', 'answerTime': 'May 26, 2009', 'unixTime': 1243321200, 'question': 'Metrolite versus frame with Graco carseat: Thoughts?', 'answer': "I had both the SafeSeat and the Snugride, and I agree, the SafeSeat is not worth the extra $$ and is way too heavy with a baby added, past 20 lbs. On a side note, I got rid of the SafeSeat after reading the big Consumer Reports article where it was the worst performer in simulated crashes (it kept coming off the base in different tests). The article was later recalled because the actual speed used in the test exceeded the minimum standard for car seats (it was closer to 70 mph if I remember). The article caused much outrage for publishing information on tests above the minimum required. Nonetheless, I couldn't help but wonder if these results didn't reflect real life conditions as I'm on the highway a lot. I decided to get the Snugride which was a top performer in that CR test, because flawed or not, all car seats were tested with the same (fast) speed. Of course, this is a personal decision (and that I had bought the Snugride before the article was recalled - I still would have if I had known before). I love the frame for the Snugride - inexpensive, light, durable. As to the stroller, I also have the Volo and love that it's super lightweight and folds very compactly. It's not suitable for an infant so your child must be able to sit up by himself. If you want an all-around lightweight stroller (12-13 lbs) that reclines, try the Maclaren Quest (good for cities, water resistant, higher maximum weight) or the Inglesina Trip (wheels are better for uneven surfaces, more waterproof, suspension). These are virtually identical in size and weight. Don't go for the Graco superdeluxe fully loaded strollers which are bulky and weight 25+ lbs. They'll take up a lot of your trunk space and are very hard to lift into a car. For convertible carseat, I had the Roundabout (goes to 40 lbs, great for small cars) and now have the Boulevard (higher weight limit) and highly recommend them. Basically, buy your strollers and car seats as you need them. Your needs will change over time and you can get last year's models for a lot less if you look."}
