{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00081L2SU', 'answerTime': 'Nov 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415088000, 'question': 'How can I get a replacement leash? On a recent trip, it got separated from the harness part and got lost.', 'answer': 'If it were up to me, I would go with something more robust in terms of leashes. I guess the main question is: how did the leash separate and get lost? My guess is that the clip on the leash broke off. Chances are good that the webbing didn\'t rip and the child didn\'t manually disconnect anything (due to anchor placement). In my opinion, your best bet is to get something more robust than the Kid Keeper\'s included leash can offer. Based on the variety of products in the market that I\'ve tested, I\'d say you have two options; the nature of your outings and travel patterns of the child will likely dictate which one would be preferred. The first is a good retractable dog leash. Most manufacturers offer product in a few different weight classes and I recommend the "large dog" range. These are rated for dogs up to 110 lbs. Not only does the webbing provide more tensile strength, but the clasps, pulleys, and handle are all robust enough to restrain a full grown golden retriever, let alone a fired-up toddler making a bee-line for the candy aisle. Should a handheld apparatus such as this not meet your needs, I suggest you go the anchor-to-anchor route. I\'m likely butchering the technical terminology, but the fact is that if you use quality climbing rope and 800+ lbs. carabiners on both ends, you can securely cement your child\'s chest rig to your own full body harness. By employing your own full body harness, you also have the added benefit of a lower center of gravity to offset the child\'s power to height ratio. Good luck!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00081L2SU', 'answerTime': 'Jul 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405407600, 'question': 'I\'m guessing the "255" number is supposed to 25? Does anyone know the the biggest measurement? I need 2 of these for a 5 and 6 year old', 'answer': 'Yes, it should be 25". I have 2.5yr olds and they fit them now but won\'t for long without me having to do some sewing. One of my boys is the avg size of a 3.5yr old. He\'s around 40lbs and almost 40in tall. If you have small for their age kiddos, these might work. If your children are avg size or bigger, I\'m betting the won\'t fit unless you add material to make it bigger. Hope this helps.'}
