{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Nov 7, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383811200, 'question': 'Can this be used when you have one wall and one wood (round) banister, without drilling holes into the bannister?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes probably. If you install it precisely. It has a tension pressure lock with rubber grommet looking things at the end that will press against the round bannister. As long as you don't have an extremely large dog jumping on it it should work. Ours presses against a bare wall and it work very well. We love it because it swings open beautiful. Hopefully it works for you."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396508400, 'question': 'When I try to push the handle down to lock it, it is very hard. The video makes locking look easy. I am afraid to force it. Am I doing something wrong or is forcing it the way to go?', 'answer': "You have to adjust the gate width, make the gate fit little looser till it closes easilly. When it feels like you can't close it, the gate is too wide for the frame. Pushing the handle down makes the gate to expand, so if you already have a tight fit, it won't close. Hope this helps."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'May 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399273200, 'question': 'What hardware comes with the unit? Is it the large hardware package or the small hardware package?', 'answer': 'There is no hardware with this unit. It is self-contained.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405407600, 'question': 'Is it very easy to open? I want to buy it for my 5 year old son so when I am not close to him and cannot supervise if he is holding the rail and walking instead of running downstairs, the gate will stop him.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The lever to release the tension requires pressure downward and lift out of the "catch" to release. I cannot picture a five year old releasing the tension to open the gate. The "locking" of the tension is the opposite - pressure downward and pushing in the "catch". The first few times I felt I might break the lever but it seems much easier now.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405494000, 'question': 'Does anyone know if its possible to hinge mount this gate and it swing back open 180 degrees?', 'answer': 'yes and yes. just dont mount it on drywall.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Nov 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1417248000, 'question': 'What are the size of the holes in the gate face? I ask because I am using it contain small animals in my kitchen for playtime and they can squeeze.', 'answer': 'Well it depends on what kind of animals you are referring to? This holes are not very big and contain children quite well. If you are talking puppies or cats then I think you are fine. But if you mean lizards or snakes then I would not recommend.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Nov 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416384000, 'question': 'gate will lock but will not retract', 'answer': 'Sometimes it sticks a little'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404802800, 'question': 'Is this gate self-standing or does require putting screws or similar into the door to support the gate?', 'answer': "It will stand on it's own, but only if you expand it to fit between two vertical surfaces. You adjust it to the width you want. Then push the plastic lever (in the middle of the gate) down and lock it. Pushing the lever down expands the gate a bit more so that it's wedged between your vertical surfaces. That holds it upright."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A796WG', 'answerTime': 'Jun 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403506800, 'question': 'if you drill into the wall does it swivel I have an uneven wall doorway?', 'answer': 'Yes, it does. It is very flexible that way.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A7SYNS', 'answerTime': 'Jun 30, 2011', 'unixTime': 1309417200, 'question': 'This item requires 14" depth under the tabletop: I was so frustrated trying to find one critical bit of information: how deeply does the attachment mechanism go *under* the table?', 'answer': "The maximum child weight for this chair is 37 pounds, from 6-36months. Also, the table top must be at least .75 inches thick and no more than 1.5 inches thick. You also shouldn't use it on a single leg table, folding tables, table extensions or glass tables."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000A7SYNS', 'answerTime': 'Jan 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389427200, 'question': 'Has anyone put the seat in the washing machine?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes. The cloth part of the seat comes off and the insert panel in the bottom comes out to just pop in the wash. We keep one of these at each bof.my in laws and have one for travel ourselves. Easy peas to wash!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A7SYNS', 'answerTime': 'Oct 1, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380610800, 'question': 'How do I fold in the Chicco Caddy Hook on Table arms in?', 'answer': "There are 2 little buttons on each arm. The one with the slot allows the arms to come off. The one behind it presses in to allow the other to move within it's slot. This allows the arms to swing towards the middle. I slide the adjustable support arms in so the unit folds more flat. Then I buckle the harness around the arms to keep it all together."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000A7SYNS', 'answerTime': 'Apr 18, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397804400, 'question': 'How do you fold it in the bag?', 'answer': "It is awkward to fold - but does fold flat. There are little push buttons that you have to hold in and fold the arms together. Mine didn't come with a bag for storage."}
