{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000H4C6FG', 'answerTime': 'Oct 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413529200, 'question': "My son's room has very low windows (2nd floor) so he would be able to reach the wedges... would he be able to pull the wedge off the velcrow?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes he would be able to pull them off. If you don't want him to be able to remove them, I would use prime line vinyl window locks. These have a toggle which allows the window to be in various stages of openness, yet secure. If you want something more permanent and stationary, you could use Cardinal gates window warden which is screwed into the frame. Hope this helps."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000H4C6FG', 'answerTime': 'Aug 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407654000, 'question': 'What are the dimensions of the wedge? We only have a 1/2" of flat surface on our window for the width. Thanks', 'answer': 'They are 3/4" wide.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000H4C6FG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405580400, 'question': 'Can you open the window past the velcro (if you remove the wedge) so you can open the window wider at times?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Yes, the Velcro does not inhibit use of the window. Just remove the wedges and you're good to go."}
