{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000HKY1GM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 31, 2011', 'unixTime': 1320044400, 'question': 'Is it level with the bed? So my question is how level with the bed is this co-speeper?', 'answer': 'there is a 5" lip and i hated that. also, it is probably only level if you get the extender legs. our bed is 26" high. the lip side of the co-sleeper was level with the mattress when folded down. so the baby\'s mattress was 5" below the level of our mattress (with extender legs). i still had to get out of bed to nurse...but also i had a C-section.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000HKY1GM', 'answerTime': 'Aug 2, 2010', 'unixTime': 1280732400, 'question': 'why is it better than a pack n play? But what is the advantage, if any, of getting this over, say, a pack n play with bassinet, which I could keep next to my bed?', 'answer': "I have had a fantastic experience with our co-sleeper. The only real difference between this and a pack n play is that it's almost the same level of your mattress, and attached, so lifting baby in and out in the middle of the night was so easy. It was a lot easier keeping my boy asleep when I didn't have to sit up and lift him over the edge of a pack n play. It's not essential, either one would work, but I found it very helpful for us. Granted, he still ends up snuggled between us half the time - but it's nice to have a spot to stick him when I want a little room to stretch. My boy's almost a year old, and on the larger end of the scale, and he's still using ours. It's snug though!"}
