{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Oct 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381734000, 'question': 'Which toddler car seats have been used in an airplane successfully? We have the Alpha Omega and measured it...and it is wider than the suggested 16".', 'answer': 'We have Costco seats that are 17.5" wide. We fit but we as a parent had to deal with the seat impeding our full seat since we had to put the arm rest on. Also walking down the aisle was a HUGE ordeal. Ask, beg, plead to be able to take your car seats on plane before everyone else boards. Otherwise you are knocking elbows.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Apr 24, 2010', 'unixTime': 1272092400, 'question': 'Travelling Toddler Straps: Would like to purchase straps, but not sure if they would attach to a car seat purchased in Australia.', 'answer': "Don't know if you've already made your trip, but here's my two cents. I push/pull my 2 year old in her seat attached to the suitcase just fine (she is about 26 lbs.). I use this strap all the time and love it. The thing I would be most concerned with is if your suitcase will support the weight. Be sure the suitcase you use has a very sturdy and strong expandable handle that secures into place when extended. Also, be sure that your suitcase has firm sides and that you pack it full so that it is a solid base for the seat and child to rest upon. When I just made a trip with my daughter, I did think that the whole bundle was quite heavy. I have a 21 inch Ricardo suitcase from Kohls (http://www.kohls.com/kohlsStore/luggagebackpacks/brandsforluggage/ricardo/PRD~586563/Ricardo+Santa+Cruz+Lite+21in+Expandable+Wheeled+CarryOn.jsp). Hope this helps. Good luck!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Dec 2, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385971200, 'question': "To make the best use of the straps onto the luggage, is there a weight limit (car seat + child's weight)? What can you tell from your experience?", 'answer': "My daughter weighs 34 pounds. I don't think this is completely meant to work with a kid in it, but I have used it this way. Also, I tend to use it with a carry on bag vs. a full size suitcase. The fuller the bag the better so it does not collapse."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Jun 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403852400, 'question': 'Has anyone used it with Recaro car seats? Does it work with Recaro?', 'answer': 'I have a Recaro ProRide Convertible, and it does seem to work as long as the luggage is very full and the strap is very tight. If the luggage isn\'t full enough, the car seat will drag, so keep this in mind when trying it out at home -- I had to stuff my luggage with pillows to make it work in practice runs. I am using a 22" carry on, not sure if anything smaller will work with such a tall car seat.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Aug 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1409122800, 'question': 'I have a britax marathon and the seat seems to be dragging on the ground. I\'ve tightened all the straps, and suitcase is 22" but dragging. Help?', 'answer': "Tightening the sides is much more important. Loosen the top and redo your fasten focusing on getting the seat lined up to the suitcase as flush as possible. Make sure the suitcase is standing straight up... maybe even tilted towards the car seat slightly. Pull the base in and up and then tighten the crap out of the side straps. The top of the car seat may be higher than your suitcase. Then tighten the top just to secure it. You have to get it tight enough around the middle so it doesn't allow the car seat to slide down or take on any additional angle. I have a G1 and G3 Marathon and I definitely have more trouble with the G3. But its mainly because of the heavy duty base with the rubber lining"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Jun 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403852400, 'question': 'I have a 20" carry-on suitcase and a Britax Marathon car seat. Would anybody know if the car seat will be too long and will drag on the bottom?', 'answer': "Just put the car seat next to your carry on suitcase and see if the top of the seat doesn't extend beyond the half - point of the extended handle. The bottom of the seat should be leveled with the bottom of the suitcase. As long as you can drag the suitcase with the handle extended, it'll be fine."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000JHN3AS', 'answerTime': 'Feb 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392624000, 'question': 'how do u connect the carseat to both end of the o-ring?', 'answer': 'The strap is shaped like an upside-down T. You use the latch hooks that go through the forward-facing belt path to hook to the left and the right rings (that go on either side of the suitcase) and the top tether to hook to the top ring (that goes over the top of the suitcase).'}
