{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000LSL5ZK', 'answerTime': 'Oct 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412146800, 'question': 'Those giving 5 star ratings - how did you handle the weight of spoon causing it to topple off the bowl?', 'answer': "Sorry, I haven't had that problem, probably for two reasons: We use these for feeding, not for letting the kids feed themselves; and our bowls are large enough in diameter that the last inch or so of the spoon's handle comes to rest on the edge of it, with the spoon part well inside the bottom of the bowl (even if it's shallow, that tends to be a safe angle); that way, the fulcrum or the balance tipping point is never far enough over to chuck the spoon out. (You can have the same problem with normal teaspoons and small-diameter fruit or jello or shallow jogurt cups. The size and the relative weight really matter.)"}
