{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P229P2', 'answerTime': 'Nov 2, 2011', 'unixTime': 1320217200, 'question': 'Any idea if this will fit into the trunk of a 2011 Prius?', 'answer': 'We just received this stroller and it fit in the hatch of my scion (I had to take off the canopy). As small as my car is I would imagine that the prius would have a bigger truck. I looked at joovy.com and when I went to the specific stroller the specifications listed the folded dimentions which helped me know it would fit.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P229P2', 'answerTime': 'Jan 23, 2013', 'unixTime': 1358928000, 'question': 'Toddler facing forward in the back? Has anyone found a safe way to face a toddler forward in the backseat?', 'answer': 'Sold on Amazon: "Joovy Caboose Too Rear Seat"'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P229P2', 'answerTime': 'Jun 25, 2009', 'unixTime': 1245913200, 'question': 'Infant carrier in the back?? Has anyone tried to put the infant carrier in the back part?', 'answer': "I would never, NEVER put the carseat in the back of this carriage... it's just not worth it. It's not the way the carriage was made or intended for use. Accidents happen...what if you were to tip the carriage up to go up a curb and the carseat fell out?!? I saw someone's review with a picture of the carseat in the back like you are describing and I thought to myself...CRAZY! Use the thing the way it was intended. Better to be safe than sorry. If you think your older child will get bored (?) give them a book to look at or play a little game with them like I Spy or soimething. Your sister doesn't even have this model carriage...don't do it. Follow the directions; it's better than a trip to the emergency room. And if you were to use it incorrectly and something went wrong; you'd look like such an incompetant when you explained how your child/children got hurt. I'm big for following directions and using things properly. Good luck!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000P229P2', 'answerTime': 'Jun 25, 2009', 'unixTime': 1245913200, 'question': 'Any tried to put the infant carrier in the back? There was no baby in it yet, however, so I was just wondering about the weight distribution if the front was empty - will it tip backwards??', 'answer': "I would never, NEVER put the carseat in the back of this carriage... it's just not worth it. It's not the way the carriage was made or intended for use. Accidents happen...what if you were to tip the carriage up to go up a curb and the carseat fell out?!? I saw someone's review with a picture of the carseat in the back like you are describing and I thought to myself...CRAZY! Use the thing the way it was intended. Better to be safe than sorry. If you think your older child will get bored (?) give them a book to look at or play a little game with them like I Spy or soimething. Your sister doesn't even have this model carriage...don't do it. Follow the directions; it's better than a trip to the emergency room. And if you were to use it incorrectly and something went wrong; you'd look like such an incompetant when you explained how your child/children got hurt. I'm big for following directions and using things properly. Good luck!"}
