{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000PGB0MQ', 'answerTime': 'Sep 20, 2009', 'unixTime': 1253430000, 'question': 'Does this seat make babies hot?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I have the Roundabout and Boulevard in Onyx (black) and the material is smooth and doesn\'t overheat as much as others on the market, even during summer months. If you baby "runs hot" (mine can get sweaty just from sleeping anywhere) there is an extra-large "seat chiller" sold at babydagny dot com that you keep in the freezer and place on the car seat minutes before putting your child in to cool it down. I have yet to use it for this car seat (don\'t need it) - it\'s really great inside coolers though!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000PGB0MQ', 'answerTime': 'May 22, 2009', 'unixTime': 1242975600, 'question': 'Britax car seat indent damage: Has anyone had a problem of this car seat ripping the cloth?', 'answer': 'Try the "Sunshine Kids Ultra-Mat Ulimate 2-Part Undermat Car Seat Mat" or the "DuoMat 2-in-1 Car Seat and Mat by Kiddopotamus" Look for padded bottom and seat cover, so that it can be used rearfacing and forward facing. Either way they protect well. Skip the ones that only cover the seat because your child will kick the cloth and it won\'t be protected.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000PGB0MQ', 'answerTime': 'Nov 30, 2008', 'unixTime': 1228032000, 'question': 'Britax Roundabout use with Maclaren Frame: Does anyone know if the Britaz Roundabout comes out of its base and can be popped onto the Maclaren Frame?', 'answer': 'no, convertible seats do not do that (except the orbit, but that only attaches to the orbit stroller)'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000PGB0MQ', 'answerTime': 'Feb 3, 2010', 'unixTime': 1265184000, 'question': "Expired 2002 Britax Roundabout: I feel like if anything was to ever happen to our child in a crash, I couldnt live with myself for using the old seat, but is it really as dangerous as my first time mother's imagination is making it seem?", 'answer': 'The other answer is 100% correct! The plastic becomes brittle over time, meaning it will break much easier in an accident. This is especially true because carseats are in extremly hot and cold conditions in the car. A carseat specialist told us that carseats in drier climates should expire after only 4 years. Hope this helps!'}
