{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000V2Y5BW', 'answerTime': 'Apr 15, 2013', 'unixTime': 1366009200, 'question': 'Why is this advertised as a baby toy since it has a warning of a choking hazard?', 'answer': "I think that the standard for the warning is if the toy has small pieces or if small pieces could be generated upon breakage. In this case, there are no chokeable parts, but if the ring were to break, I could see how there would be chokeable parts generated. It would take more than usual baby play to break this ring, though. As an adult, I'd have to make a concerted effort to break it, and my first step would be to open the ring, which even my toddler can't do. I don't think it's reasonably a choking hazard when played with by babies/toddlers. That said, my children haven't found it to be a fun toy or a particularly good teether. If you haven't bought it yet, skip it."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000V2Y5BW', 'answerTime': 'Feb 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392883200, 'question': 'Is this toy BPA free?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "Ashley, I didn't know, so I asked. This is the reply: Thank you for your inquiry, The First Keys, Stacking Cups and Soft Teething Beads are polypropylene number 5 on the recycling plastics list and does not contain BPA, PVC or Phthalates. Thank you Consumer Services customerservice@learningcurve.com"}
