{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000VVIHPS', 'answerTime': 'Jun 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1371193200, 'question': 'I cannot get the clock itself to work. It just says 12:00 and will not change no matter which button I push. Please help.', 'answer': 'Try this: Unplug it, wait a few seconds and plug it in. Press the SET button and then the up or down arrow button. I have to wear glasses during this part because the writing is so tiny, but along the left side you should see it switching from AM to PM to 00(military time), every time you push the up or down arrow. Once you\'ve got it to what you want, then press set again. Now you use the up or down arrows to set the minute of what time it is RIGHT NOW. Then press set again and use the up or down arrows to set the hour of what time it is RIGHT NOW. Press set again and go through the same routine to set the minute and hour of your desired wake up time. This will become the time when the "sleeping bunny" becomes the "wake up" bunny. There\'s one last set of times you can set but i\'ve never used that feature. I just press set until it goes back to the time you originally set which is the time it is now. That\'s it! If that doesn\'t work, it may be broken.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B000VVIHPS', 'answerTime': 'Sep 2, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378105200, 'question': 'Is it appropriate for a 3 year old', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000VVIHPS', 'answerTime': 'Jan 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388908800, 'question': 'Does the "wake" setting last until you turn it off, or does it go off automatically. If I set for 6am - will it still be on if she wakes at 7am?', 'answer': 'It\'s supposed to stay "on" in that example. However, our clock was changing from on to off and vice versa at random times, causing major confusion for everyone. The concept is a good idea but if it doesn\'t work perfectly, you\'re worse off. My son was seriously confused that his bunny clock was on at 2am, for example (if he happened to glance at it). And then getting rid of the broken clock was an issue for him. I would skip it and get a digital clock, as we did. My son is 3 and now knows what 7am looks like.'}
