{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000YJ89K0', 'answerTime': 'Sep 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411110000, 'question': 'Can the child sit on this board as well as stand? Also, any idea if it would fit an InStep Safari in-line double jogger?', 'answer': 'My kid would sit on it but it\'s not safe. She also had to either sit cross legged so her feet wouldn\'t drag or she would place her feet onto the bars of the MacClaren. I don\'t know the stroller you are referring to but the board is really adjustable in regard to width & arm length. You just need to make sure to have two clear bars (about 2" of space) to attach the arms to it. My friend used this with her city mini double stroller and it was fine. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000YQMWLU', 'answerTime': 'Jun 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1370329200, 'question': 'i am a size 36 d what size nipple sheld should i get', 'answer': "The size is not about your bra size but about your nipple size. I wear a 34 d and used a 20 mm nipple shield because I have small nipples. Medium is 24 mm which is pretty standard as to what they hand out at hospitals and such. But that was too big for me. You really just need to get 2 sizes to see what works best for you. If you think you're pretty small get a small and medium, if you think you're large get a medium and large. Good luck"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000YQMWLU', 'answerTime': 'Oct 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414220400, 'question': 'Better to be really snug fit around nipple or have a little extra room in there? Tried on two sizes, not sure which to get more of.', 'answer': "It's better to have a snug fit. First, if there is too much room there is more air in the shield and your baby will have to suck air before they get any milk, which makes a gassy, cranky baby. Second, the snug fit means that when baby latches it will have more contact with the actual nipple and keep up stimulation so you won't lose as much milk production. Even if it's a snug fit it will still protect your nipple from abrasion, but if it's too big you will find it harder to switch baby back to breast later."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000YQMWLU', 'answerTime': 'Apr 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396940400, 'question': 'For those of you with flat nipples, were you able to wean off the nipple shield? If so, when and how did you do it?', 'answer': "I would offer breast before feeding and she would get frustrated what worked for us is offering bare breast when switching to second breast. This kept baby from getting upset fast and in turn helped with my anxiety. When she started to get a hold of it (a few days) I started to just offer bare breast first and one day she just latched and we haven't used it since."}
{'answer': 'Soft flexible', 'asin': 'B000YQMWLU', 'answerTime': '1 day ago', 'question': 'Are these hard or soft flexible shields?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000YQMWLU', 'answerTime': 'Feb 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391760000, 'question': 'help with let down?', 'answer': 'More with latching problems or sore nipples'}
