{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000ZKHVMU', 'answerTime': 'Nov 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416124800, 'question': "My hi-rise apt bldg has commercial-like toilets (without tank + std. residential plumbing) - how do I connect? Can I connect to sink H2O just 2' away?", 'answer': "Yes and no: You CAN connect to your sink, but you'll probably (almost definitely) need an adapter or two, and you might need to connect where in a cabinet or something. Also keep in mind you'll be knocking poop into your sink; it won't always wash right down and could ultimately clog your drain. Backspray might also wind up places you don't want it. That said, there *should* be some access point somewhere to your toilet for emergency shut-offs. Sometimes there is a panel behind or near the toilet that will be covering the shut-off valve. There's a shut-off somewhere, you just need to find it, although it might be inconveniently far from the toilet itself. You might also considering hooking up to the sink in your kitchen if you have a disposal; same problem with reach and cabinets and adapters, but worth consideration. For that matter, you can hook up to ANY pipe bringing water into the house; just needs to be fitted and practical."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000ZKHVMU', 'answerTime': 'Feb 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392364800, 'question': "Is this product worth it if baby is already on solids and their poop is more solid? Or best for the 'runnier' breastmilk poop?", 'answer': "I installed this before our first baby was born. Then found out that completely breast-fed poop is water soluble, so need to remove it before washing. We didn't use the sprayer while our baby was only breast fed. Now, he is getting into solids and we've started using the sprayer is is very powerful if you need it to be and more helpful for solids then breast fed."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B000ZKHVMU', 'answerTime': 'Feb 13, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392278400, 'question': "Is this product worth it if baby is already on solids and their poop is more solid? Or best for the 'runnier' breastmilk poop?", 'answer': 'If you\'re using cloth diapers now and you feel the need to spray them out, this is good. If your baby makes a "clean" poop that just falls out and leaves little on the diaper, you won\'t really need this. In our house, we used it on maybe every other poopy diaper. It just helps the diapers come cleaner and the pale doesn\'t get as funky. We washed our diapers daily but I\'ve heard some people did every other day and I think if you have diapers in a pail 2 days, you\'ll want to spray them. Regardless, if you decide to buy blueberries, you\'re gonna need it. :)'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00101TXPG', 'answerTime': 'Sep 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1379401200, 'question': 'can you turn down the "mic" volume so that it doesn\'t pick up every tiny sound?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The only volume you can adjust is on the parent unit. What I do is turn off the monitor in my kids room every Night before bed. It seems to amplify sound the longer the kids unit is on. Hope this helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00101TXPG', 'answerTime': 'Jan 20, 2015', 'unixTime': 1421740800, 'question': 'does anyone know if I am able to buy the battery pack and dc adaptor cord for this unit? We have the monitor, but not cord for parent unit', 'answer': "I don't think so. But you can buy a used system on Ebay for $9.99 which is probably less than those parts would cost if they were sold separately. You could then use the parts from it."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00101TXPG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 31, 2014', 'unixTime': 1406790000, 'question': 'why is my monitor too loud', 'answer': 'Short answer: try turning it off and then on again every single day. Mine worked great for about a week and then became deafening loud even on the lowest volume setting. I discovered that if I reset the child-end of the monitor, it works great again for about 24 hours before become ridiculously loud again.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00101TXPG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398668400, 'question': 'Can you have two of these monitors in the same house without them interfering with each other?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "With the two I have, I don't have to worry about them interfering with each other because everything else on the planet seems to interfere with them. A big waste of money on my part."}
