{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Jun 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402815600, 'question': 'Does this have the new buckle after the recall?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, has new buckle. Models produced after 8/19/2013 have new buckle. We purchased in Feb 2014. Seat was manufactured in Dec 2013 and had the new buckle. We ordered the recall anyway and the replacement buckle was identical.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Aug 6, 2011', 'unixTime': 1312614000, 'question': 'Can my 16 month old use the Graco Nautilus 3-in-1 car seat? Ive been reading some reviews and ppl are stressing not to use this car seat till your child is 3 yrs old but my child defiently fits the weight limit and height reqirements so I dont know...', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I am a car seat tech, and an ER RN. I have seen kids become quadriplegics due to forward facing at young ages. My almost 4 yr old and my 28 month old are still rear facing and will be until they are 45 lbs. It would be my expert opinion that your child will be up to 4 times safer if kept rear facing. As a parent, please consider their safety. It is easy to keep them rear facing if it's all they know. You can get a seat that will rear face up for 40 lbs and harness to 65 lbs. That would be my suggestion for you. The newest recommendations from the AAP is to keep kids rear facing until age 2 or they reach the weight limit of their convertible seat, whichever is LONGER. Motor vehicle collisions are the leading cause of PREVENTABLE deaths of kids in the US. And there has never been a documented case of leg injury from extended rear facing. Please don't think I am preaching, I just try to do what I can to keep kids safe! Any questions, feel free to ask."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Sep 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411974000, 'question': 'Can I use the car seat on a car which does not have a latch system?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'This car seat is equipped with dual auto seat fastener options. You can either use the latch or seat belt. I use both but either can serve individually.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Oct 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381561200, 'question': 'What is the padding like on the bottom, going for a 16 hour road trip? Thanks!', 'answer': 'I wish the padding on my own seat was as good as the padding on the Graco Nautilus.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Dec 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1417420800, 'question': 'Would you recommend using at 1 years, forwards? Now son is 10 months, 20lbs and 30in...I wonder how soon I could convert directly to this - thoughts?', 'answer': "No, absolutely not. Children need to be rear facing until a minimum of age two. Check out these links about why rear facing until two is imperative for your baby's safety! http://csftl.org/why-rear-facing-the-science-junkies-guide/ http://csftl.org/rear-facing-car-seat-myths-busted/ https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q8gU9zzCGA8 https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sssIsceKd6U"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0011URFRE', 'answerTime': 'Nov 3, 2011', 'unixTime': 1320303600, 'question': 'Recline Problem? Does this seem to be an issue that anyone else is having?', 'answer': "The Graco Nautilus is a good car seat all in all, but has bad reclining feature. I agree, the recline feature seems to be only for fitting it into your car, not for the comfort of your child. My son is 2 years, and we keep struggling with his head bobbing around with every turn or when the car slows down (momentum pulls his head forward) - poor guy... You may want to try it out in the car you're fitting it in to see if it helps to adjust the car seat while the grace is fixed into it."}
