{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Jun 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403161200, 'question': 'Can I use this with my 18 month old child who is ~28lbs? Or should I use a full car seat?', 'answer': "Depends on how your toddler is. I started using it with my son at 23 months. The decision didn't have to do with age or weight, it was just his age when we first purchased him his own seat. I wouldn't hesitate to use it for my daughter who is currently 20 months. I don't know her weight, but it's about the same as your child. I find this much easier to use than lugging a car seat through the airport. It's guaranteed to fit in the plane seat, which isn't always true for regular car seats. One warning, you have to distract the child from the plane belt buckle. At first, they want to keep undoing the seatbelt. My son got over it rather quickly (or I flipped the buckle so he couldn't lift the flap)."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Feb 13, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392278400, 'question': 'Will it work for First Class seat? Seats are a bit larger, is there a lot of slack in the harness. 2.3 yr old petite @28 lbs :) thanks!', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I would think so. The strap that goes around the back of the seat is adjustable.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Aug 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407394800, 'question': 'My child is 10 months old but weighs 22 pounds and is 28" tall. Any reason I cannot use this for her?', 'answer': 'None I can think of. My little one started using it around the same height and weight. Remember... There is no between the legs strap. I advise you to use one of those grippy mat jar opener things to put on the seat to help prevent any sliding.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Dec 18, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387353600, 'question': 'My son is four years old, 35 pounds but 42 inches tall. Can he still use this even though he is two inches over the 40 inch cut off?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The only issue I can see with this would be that because the bottom hooks into the airplane seatbelt the straps might not be tall enough and then they might cut into his shoulders. I guess it depends more on how tall your son's torso is when sitting up. I can measure my seatbelt when I get home tonight to see how tall the straps are..."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'May 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399359600, 'question': 'I will be traveling on a 787 Dreamliner. Does anybody have experience using this system on this plane? I do not want the strap to obstruct trays or screens behind me.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'When you receive the package with this harness in it, it directs you to a video you can view on how to set it up and use it. It is very instructive and clear. All you do is ask the person behind you to lower their tray and you put the strap around the seat and then they put the tray back up and obstructs nothing. Our grandson is so used to being buckled into his car seat, and this mimics that strapping, that he thought nothing of being strapped in and then sitting still the entire flight.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Jan 21, 2015', 'unixTime': 1421827200, 'question': 'We are flying from NY to Australia with a 2 kids, will the harness interfere with the other passengers tray tables on Qantas planes?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'Most planes have the tray table "recessed" into the seat in front of them. Usually families travelling with children are allowed to board first. You just have to lower the tray table behind the child, slip the strap in place and tighten then return their tray table "to the locked" position. The belt will not interfere with them opening or lowering their table during flight. Not sure how you confirm the plane you\'re traveling on has the tray tables recessed. I used mine for 3 years on probably a dozen flight (internationally as well) and never found a tray table not recessed...but I\'ve heard some planes don\'t have recessed tables. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Aug 7, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407394800, 'question': 'Many say kid slips out the bottom without a workaround. Is this true? If so how is this safer than just going as a lap child?', 'answer': "I suppose, depending on the type of impact, that a child could slip out the bottom. However, that's unlikely in the event of turbulence, where the child would tend to fly upward, but the harness would hold them down by their shoulders, or in a severe frontal impact, where the child's upper body would be held back against the seats by the shoulder straps. Crash studies have shown that it's difficult for a parent to maintain grip of a lapchild and keep them from flying out of the parent's arms in the event of sudden deceleration or unexpected turbulence."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0012E4FV8', 'answerTime': 'Nov 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416211200, 'question': "Does this harness work if you're sitting in the back seat? I know you have to put in tray behind your child's seat...thank you!", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes'}
