{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380870000, 'question': "How do you reduce clumping while mixing? I've tried formula first, mix a little at a time, what am I doing wrong?", 'answer': 'This is what my daughter tells me. Make sure locking blade is locked in right at level of liquid. She says she makes 32 oz at a time and it takes a bit to make sure the blade is pulling and churning the clumps from bottom up. Again, the key is to make sure there is no air and lock blade no higher than liquid. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381042800, 'question': "Can this pitcher be sterilized in boiling water for 10 min? I didn't see that guidance in the instructions and worry about boiling plastic.", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'We sterilize with the microwave. You put a little bit of water in the bottom and then nuke it on high for 4 minutes. Seems to be ok with that.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Nov 16, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384588800, 'question': 'since this is to prepare baby formula, do you need to sterilize the pitcher as well?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I would just sterilize after the initial purchase. You really don't need to sterilize bottles/parts each time anymore. That is an old recommendation from when water supplies weren't as safe. I do sterilize my bottles/parts once in a while still but really just hot soapy water or a run through the dishwasher is sufficient. Plus if you think about it- When you sterilize an item you also need to sterilize the tongs used to pick it up, the counter or drying rack you sit it on, the cupboard which it then goes into, and any other surface it is likely to touch by the time it's used for a feeding... You are never going to be able to keep your bottles sterile."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Dec 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1417680000, 'question': 'Does it have mL markings on the side or only ounce markers?', 'answer': 'It had both measurements'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Jul 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404370800, 'question': 'Is this pitcher microwave safe? did somebody found information from Dr. Brown? thanks.', 'answer': "I would personally never microwave any type of plastic because of the harmful chemicals that it releases into whatever you're cooking. I am not aware of any manufacturer reccomendation on it, but my pitcher seems to have warped just from using hot water, from the tap, to clean it. I'd imagine a microwave might heat it to the point it warps..."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Sep 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411974000, 'question': 'Has anyone used this pitcher with GERBER formula? For whatever reason, gerber clumps and I have tried everything so hopefully this pitcher works.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'We use Gerber Good Start Gentle and I fortify the formula to 24kcal (so there is more powder per ml). Before buying this pitcher we always had clumps or formula stuck to the bottom of our measuring cup. This mixing pitcher has allowed us to prepare formula in large amounts with no clumps or residue left in the bottom of the pitcher. My best purchase as a new mother!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Oct 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412233200, 'question': 'My dr recommended mixing 1/2 formula with 1/2 breastmilk due to supply issues. Could I do that with this mixing pitcher?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Sure! I'm doing 1/2 & 1/2 with whole milk as we are weening. Just make up your 1/2 formula (less than 1/2 of what the pitcher can hold) and then add your 1/2 BM. Give it a few pumps to mix and you should be good to go."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Mar 25, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427266800, 'question': "Once you've prepared a pitcher of formula, do the bottles need to be reshaken then before being given to baby?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I don't reshake the bottles, I just pour into a bottle and warm the bottle up. Sometimes I swirl it around a little to make sure it's evenly heated but that's it! I haven't noticed any settling. And haven't had any problems! I've been using it daily for two months now."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 27, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425024000, 'question': 'Riddle me this! Say I mix 16oz water with 8 Scoops and the result is 18oz of total liquid to pour into bottles, is baby getting 18oz or 16???', 'answer': '18oz. :).'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001I481LM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 27, 2015', 'unixTime': 1425024000, 'question': 'After you make it, do you heat it up by putting the bottle in a bowl of warm water for feedings?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Not the whole pitcher of course, but yes, individual bottles can be warmed up in hot water or a bottle warmer.'}
