{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OB83FS', 'answerTime': 'Dec 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387958400, 'question': 'How wide is the actual walk thru part?', 'answer': 'It is 17". I have two for potty training a rescue puppy. It is so easy to use that I plan on keeping them up indefinitely even though she is now one. If I don\'t need to use them, they stay open, but if I have company or Contractors I can create zones. Or if I don\'t need her help to wash the kitchen floor. I don\'t try to carry a dirty cloths hamper or large garbage bags through.. The garbage bags I lift over the gate.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001OB83FS', 'answerTime': 'Aug 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407481200, 'question': 'Does the door stay open easily (without being tied)? I only want it closed when I leave the house.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "If it's mounted tilted slightly backward,MIT will stay open easily."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001OB83FS', 'answerTime': 'Aug 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407308400, 'question': 'Will the gate fit a 49" wide opening?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'The manufacturer states 48" maximum. Two possible solutions with this gate, which we like a lot, are as follows. One would be to buy two gates,as there are multiple extensions, which might work, but would be expensive unless you need two gates one opening 49" and one less than 45". The other solution,which should work, would be to get two 3/4" boards that are at least 34" long and at least 2" wide to place next to each wall and the gate pressure would hold them in place. They could be painted the wall color. We use them in 35", 36.5" and 39 " openings so there is definitely flexibility. Hope these thoughts help!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001OB83FS', 'answerTime': 'Jul 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405148400, 'question': 'Is the walk through door able to swing open both ways?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes it does.'}
