{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OC5UMQ', 'answerTime': 'Dec 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386748800, 'question': "How wide is the actual swing door? Afraid my big hips won't fit through :)", 'answer': 'The gate opening is 16" at 30" above the floor when the gate is installed so that the bottom rail is touching the floor. I would take a tape measure and put the tip on the floor, measure up 30" next to your thigh (or hip or rear end as the case may be) and put your finger on that spot. Then I would measure across your thighs (hips or rear) from that spot. If it is greater than 16" you are going to have to squeeze through the gate and might consider an alternative.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001OC5UMQ', 'answerTime': 'Oct 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413356400, 'question': 'if the bottom has molding, would the pressure mount be adjustable for a narrower opening at bottom, versus wider opening at the top of a staircase? t', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes it would be adjustable for a narrower openening at the bottom (and vice versa) because the top and bottom can be adjusted independently from each other down to 34" with the extension and 24.5" without the extension. For example: the top can be at 37" and the bottom could be at 34 (and vice versa.)" Hope that helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OC5UMQ', 'answerTime': 'Jan 1, 2013', 'unixTime': 1357027200, 'question': 'Anyone know the amount of space between the bars, in inches? (Trying to see if my kitten will be able to slip through or not)', 'answer': 'At the widest where the bars are bent at the gate, it\'s 2.5 " At the wall, there is a narrow space of 4 " before it angles down to the narrow width, with a great possibility of kitten getting head stuck. There a full grown cat might be able to wiggle through the 2.5, it would be a challenge with the danger of them getting stuck. I wouldn\'t recommend for cats, they are curious and will try anything.'}
