{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Jul 28, 2011', 'unixTime': 1311836400, 'question': 'Fit on a shopping cart??', 'answer': "It's not supposed to go on any part of the top of a shopping part, including over the basket. It sounds like you're talking about having the seat straddle the basket of the cart, which is just as dangerous. Sure, it *could* fall into the basket, a mere 8 inches. Or it could be knocked off completely. Carts are not designed to have items placed on top of them, they become top heavy. Please reference the American Academy of Pediatrics: http://aappolicy.aappublications.org/cgi/content/full/pediatrics;118/2/e545 If anything, put the car seat *in* the basket (or wear your child, have someone else come along, etc). I know its a pain, but so is using a car seat in the first place (on top of a million other child safety measures) and you wouldn't compromise a child's safety in the car just for mere convenience..."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Nov 5, 2011', 'unixTime': 1320476400, 'question': 'is this a rear/forward facing carseat? meaning that she might fit in there even when she hits 12,14 months? Help?', 'answer': 'No. All infant car seats are rear-facing only. If you want a car seat you can switch to forward facing later, look at convertible car seats. They are designed to do this. Also the new recommendations from the American Pediatric Association, I believe, are to keep your child rear facing for 2 years, and the higher weight capacity of 30 lbs is designed to help you do that with this seat.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Feb 21, 2013', 'unixTime': 1361433600, 'question': 'Are there any Keyfit 30 systems manufactured in 2012? I ordered one and received it yesterday, and it was manufactured in 8/2011!', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It's pretty much luck of the draw what your mfr date is. Mine was manufactured 3 months before I purchased. If you are not satisfied, contact the seller and request one with a more recent date."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Mar 1, 2011', 'unixTime': 1298966400, 'question': 'Chicco Travel System Discovery and Adventure which is a newer model? My question is which model is newer Discovery or Adventure?', 'answer': "Discovery and Adventure are not models they are the color style. Chicco technically only makes one model of their travel system, which is called the Cortina. It is available with a KeyFit or KeyFit30 infant car seat, the difference being that the KeyFit is only recommended up to a 22lb baby, and the KeyFit30 will last until your baby is 30lbs. To answer your question a little better, I believe that Discovery is exclusive to the KeyFit and Adventure is exclusive to the KeyFit30. I personally would recommend the KeyFit30 it is slightly more expensive, but most convertible car seats that you would purchase as the next step for your baby have a minimum weight of 30-35lbs and you don't want your baby outgrowing his KeyFit carseat at 22lbs and be stuck without a seat or using a seat that doesn't fit him for such a period of time."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Jul 8, 2012', 'unixTime': 1341730800, 'question': "Removable Child Tray? Does anyone know if the child's tray on this model is removable (as with the Chicco Lite Stroller)?", 'answer': 'Yes, it is'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Oct 12, 2012', 'unixTime': 1350025200, 'question': "Adjustable Handle? Does the handle on the stroller adjust, or is it high enough for a taller person (6'4) to push comfortably?", 'answer': 'The stroller handle adjusts up an down very easily. There are buttons on both sides that you push in and then move the handle. My husband is 6\' 2" and I am 5\' 2" so this was very important to us. We have this stroller and it is really easy for us to both use it.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001OD6YS4', 'answerTime': 'Nov 16, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384588800, 'question': "I've used this car seat rear facing for a year and a half. Can I use it front facing as well or is it ONLY for rear facing?", 'answer': 'This seat is only to be used for rear facing. Studies show infants and toddlers should remain rear facing until at least age 2 or even up to three. Doing this will greatly reduce the risk of serious injury during accidents.'}
