{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Oct 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381993200, 'question': 'My son is 2.5 years old and am thinking for purchasing this for a plane ride and extra car seat. Is this seat too small for a 2.5 year old?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I used this seat until my oldest turned 4 when I moved him into a high back booster. I think he actually prefers the Combi seat to the booster he moved into, but he hit the weight/height limitations recently. As far as taking it on a plane I always take 1 of my Combi Coccoro seats on with me. I have a 20mo old and a 6mo old who will take turns in the seat depending on who is napping because the cushion makes it more comfortable to sleep in than the plane seat. The seat fits very easily into the plane seat also, so no struggle there. We fly internationally quite frequently and this has been a lifesaver for me on those long flights when it's difficult and uncomfortable to hold a heavy sleeping baby (or 2!) the whole way."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Jun 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1370934000, 'question': 'Does this work well/fit well in trucks?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes. I have used it in the back seat of two trucks, rear facing without any problems.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Mar 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1362556800, 'question': 'Good but light weight and inexpensivem car seat for air travel?', 'answer': "Check out the Cosco Scenera. It's super lightweight. The only issue I've found using it on the plane is that you have to be in the bulkhead seats to rear-face it but other than that it's a great, cheap, lightweight seat. I think it weighs about 8 or 9 lbs and depending on the version you get it's between $40 and $60 so you can't get cheaper."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Oct 30, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383116400, 'question': "Amazon says that the minimum weight for this seat is five pounds, but Combi's website says three pounds. Which is correct?", 'answer': 'My twins weight 4 lbs. I put them in these and they were cozy. Most sites recommend 5lbs for any convertible carseat , but it does state in their manual 3lbs. It comes with a special padding to support smaller infants, but a mom knows if she should risk it or not. I had no problems.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Jul 30, 2013', 'unixTime': 1375167600, 'question': "Is it 17 inches wide or 14.6 inches wide? I'm a little confused, sorry but gotta double check", 'answer': 'The specifications of the Coccoro are as follows (it is a pretty small carseat and fits with a toddler seat in the back of our Prius just fine): Dimensions: 17" L x 15.50" W x 28.25" H Product weight: 11.75 lbs. Rear-facing weight capacity: 3-33 lbs. Forward-facing weight capacity: 1 year and 20-40 lbs. Height allowance: 40"'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Aug 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407308400, 'question': 'Would you recommend this seat for triplet newborns? Three in a row while rear-facing.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I have twins. I strongly recommend the Graco Snugride 30 Infant Carrier until they are at least six months. You will not want to wake sleeping babies when you get home! And the infant support on the Combi pushes small babies heads forward, which is not good for NICU babies. I speak from experience...I have twins, one was in NICU, and used to foster newborns. I have never had a baby under ten pounds that fit well in this. Three across would be hard to buckle, unless you can reach through the trunk or over the top from the front seat. The buckle pieces have to be overlapped to snap in together at the crotch. That being said, we love this seat for small spaces! My first born used this seat in our sports car for several years. Very light weight and convenient. Can be a pain to install rear facing, if you don't have a tether point. If you buy these, you will want to go to a car seat installation station (check with your hospital). Also, I'll warn you that your babies will likely outgrow these seats by height before they are old enough to use a booster. I ended up buying Britax Boulevards for mine. They have higher quality fabric that is way easier to wipe to clean (you do not want to have to remove the covers when they poop through their clothes or spit up). They also work fine for tiny babies, if you absolutely don't want to purchase infant carriers. Three across all rear facing, all forward facing, and any combination of rear/forward works fine with Britax in our GMC Terrain. Combis are slightly narrower and take up less front to back space, so if you're driving a tiny car, these are for you! If you have a retailer near you, most baby stores will let you take them to the car to try them out. I got a great deal on my Graco carriers at Kohls w/cashback and 30% off. Amazon does occasionally have great deals in the open box section, too. (My other must purchase items for multiples are an Ergobaby Backpack and Podee Feeding System) Congratulations! It will get easier around six months!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Nov 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384243200, 'question': "What makes this convertible? It says weights 5-40lbs, but is that rear facing only? What's the max for front facing?", 'answer': 'The Combi Coccoro is 3-33 lbs rear facing. Forward facing maximum is 40 lbs.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B001RABUDI', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2013', 'unixTime': 1361865600, 'question': 'does Combi sell the seat covers just by themselves for if someone wants to change just the cover to a different color for an opposite sex baby?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'They absolutely do sell carseat covers. I have ordered 2 from them. Very affordable. I believe I paid 33 dollars for a new carseat cover only, I did not need the infant insert as my daughter is old enough to not use. You can call the customer service number on their website. I found them to be very helpful.'}
