{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00208IO2G', 'answerTime': 'Sep 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1410764400, 'question': 'is the "padding" thick enough to cushion a head bump. The edge of our crib has a lip and when our baby stands up he bangs his head. How thick?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Maybe 1/2 an inch. My toddler sometimes bangs her head on the front rail when she is mad. She doesn't seem phased by that fact. I would think it would seem less cushiony than a bumper the doctors tell parents not to put in the cribs. Hope this helps."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00208IO2G', 'answerTime': 'Mar 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427353200, 'question': "Okay so the big question!!! Has your baby learned to untie these? If so, don't these pose a similar risk as bumpers if they came off and fell in crib?", 'answer': "So I didn't use this until my son was about 10 months old. At that point he could stand on his own and move all around. He has never tried to untie them, though I am sure he could. To me at that point it was no more dangerous than the blanket he sleeps with every night. The big risk for suffocation with bumpers and things is that the infant is not able to roll over or move so that they can breathe. Truthfully you do not need this before they can pull up and stand and eat the furniture and at that point the risk of suffocation is so much lower it has never been a concern. This is a great product and looks great on my crib, and is way better than him chewing and eating the paint on the crib. And it doesn't damage your crib like the gummy teething guards do."}
