{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002CGRTFY', 'answerTime': 'Jan 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389772800, 'question': 'The banister I would like to use this on is angled (becomes shorter). Would I be able to cut this to size?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes. We did and it looks great. I can't say it was particularly easy though."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002CGRTFY', 'answerTime': 'Sep 30, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380524400, 'question': 'Can this be used outdoors on the balcony?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It is up to you. It could be used outdoors as all the components are plastic sheeting held up with plastic zip ties. The ends of the sheeting are held in with galvanized screws so as long as you have a stud or rigid siding you could screw the ends in no problem. The galvanization on the screws should last a reasonable amount of time. If they do rust prematurely, the fasteners are items you could get at a home improvement store for less than $2. I dont know how easy it would be to clean... I'm sure you could just use some soap/cleaner and a garden hose if it got dirty though."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002CGRTFY', 'answerTime': 'Dec 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1418630400, 'question': 'Holes in wall? Is there a way to safely install without having to make any holes in our walls? Or for the best strength, would I secure to wall?', 'answer': "Our banister has posts on either end, so we just zip-tied the ends of the plastic to the post in lieu of holes in the wall. Works great! You could probably get away with zip-tying the closet post to the wall (if you don't have/I can't fit a zip tie around end post), provided it's close enough to the wall that a kid couldn't push through (difficult feat). Advise using zip ties that match post color: dark or brown of clear on brown, for example ties vs white on brown-blends in better. Also, fasten zip ties on the side the child won't be on to avoid sharp edges left when zip ties are trimmed at the end."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002CGRTFY', 'answerTime': 'Mar 31, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427785200, 'question': 'Could this be used to enclose a TV stand in my living room or would it be too bendy to be useful?', 'answer': "It might work. It's very flexible left to right (so you can wrap it around something), but not as much top to bottom (fairly stiff that way). If the material is fixed to something on both sides (left and right edges), I don't think a small child would be able to press buttons, etc., through the material. But being able to affix it something on both edges is fairly critical. One other thing to keep in mind with small kids -- the edges can be quite sharp when cut, if not cut smoothly."}
