{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': 'Mar 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395730800, 'question': 'What ages would you suggest using this cup for? Would a one year old (most likely) have success using it?', 'answer': "Yes. My son has had them since he was about 10 months. If they drink from a straw well, it should work. If not, they have a sippy cup silicone attachment that you can use too. I'll have to warn you that these are NOT leak-proof, so I would just stick with water to easier deal with spills. Hope that helps!"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': 'May 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401433200, 'question': 'Is the picture in the description considered to be the "straw" phase? That hard plastic spout would be too hard for my 6 month old to drink from.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The picture is showing the spout. If you look closely, the light orange straw is laying in front of the sippy and behind the 3 alternate stoppers. Also, the straw fits inside the lid and goes down into the liquid without extending out of the sippy, so the plastic spout is always the same.'}
{'answer': 'No.', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': '21 days ago', 'question': 'If liquid is left in this and it gets miss placed will that cause it to rust?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': 'Nov 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415001600, 'question': 'Which bottle brush works well for cleaning this sippy cup?', 'answer': "Any bottle brush is good. Just make sure to get one that has a nipple brush as well. I definitely don't recommend this sippy cup tho. My dalighter won't even use it if I have the top of it on. You have to suck really hard to get anything to come out. I've recycled the lid and and sippy part of it and just use the stainless steel part as a regular cup, I just added a metal straw from ecojarz"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414047600, 'question': 'Is it true that you have to open up the straw and put in a valve every time you want it not to leak when out?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "You're referring to the travel plug which is included with the cup. I haven't used the travel plug at all. My son drinks all day long, so I don't worry about it. If you leave the cup alone for a while, and it's full, the liquid will rise through the straw and may leak onto the table/floor, etc. whenever I see that happening, I sip from his cup and it's a non-issue."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002DN8HCK', 'answerTime': 'Oct 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413961200, 'question': "Have you experienced these issues that reviewers complained about; straw leaks, it's hard to get water out after a few sips?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, straw definitely leaks!'}
