{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'Jun 25, 2010', 'unixTime': 1277449200, 'question': "The Rock &amp; Play Sleeper - when baby gets wet? I've registered for this because of the amazing reviews, but have one question... if the baby is wet and the sleeper gets wet, what do you do?", 'answer': "I use a waterproof sheet saver http://www.amazon.com/American-Baby-Company-Organic-Waterproof/dp/B001KZH6AG/ref=sr_1_1?ie=UTF8&s=baby-products&qid=1277471707&sr=1-1. It's the perfect size and ties right on to the four corners of the rocker. Also, the cover itself is easily removable and washable, but the sheet saver is more convenient."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'May 9, 2011', 'unixTime': 1304924400, 'question': 'Transitioning to crib - now what? What kind of wedge do you think we should buy?', 'answer': "We are using a wedge in the crib for the past three weeks and it looks like we can take it out now. Dexbaby Safe Lift Universal Crib Wedge, White When we first switched our now 5-month old she had a hard time as well. But now she's sleeping through the night. Here's what we did. We used the wedge and also put her in a sleep sack and swaddled her arms only for the first few days. We started putting her in the crib for naps only for the first week and then gradually put her in there during the night. We have a 2 1/2 year old who sleeps in the same room so night time was a little more challenging. I didn't want the baby to wake up every 45 minutes and wake up her brother. After awhile we got rid of the swaddle. She still sleeps in the sleep sack. I will soon get rid of the wedge as well because I always find her sleeping sideways towards the end of the bed in the morning. I think the wedge is good as a transitional item. We kept an eye on her with a video monitor to make sure she's safe. Good luck to all of you."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'Jul 24, 2010', 'unixTime': 1279954800, 'question': 'Rock &amp; Play - bog enough for 4-5mo.old? Airplane travel? Anyone use this on a larger baby4-5 months old?', 'answer': 'Hello there! Our "little" man is 3.5 months, 25" and 18 pounds. We use the RNP and aren\'t sure how much longer we will be able to use it. It only goes up to 25 pounds, but your child could outgrow it for length. We still have some head room, but we are thinking he will have transition by the end of August as we use this for his primary sleeping space due to acid reflux. So, you might be cutting it close if you decided to get it. Also, it travels VERY easily! We have taken it on many trips. It breaks down into a few pieces and could fit into a large suitcase. Have you looked at using a peapod?? KidCo PeaPod Portable Travel Bed with Air Pump - Red In any case, have a great time on your trip!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'May 5, 2011', 'unixTime': 1304578800, 'question': 'Can you swaddle the baby when in this sleeper? Is it possible to do so when in this sleeper?', 'answer': "There is no way the baby will wriggle out of this when swaddled. The way they are reclined in the sleeper it's just not possible to do. Our 3 months old sleeps the best when swaddled in her Rock n Play sleeper! It's the best!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'Jan 10, 2011', 'unixTime': 1294646400, 'question': 'Head Support: Do people feel an extra support is necessary or is it ok without additional support?', 'answer': "I think the recline was steep enough that our son didn't need it, even when he was a brand newborn baby. Now that he's 6 months, he definitely doesn't need any extra support. He always liked to turn his head to the side, but since the rocker doesn't hold them upright, it didn't make his head sag."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002M77N22', 'answerTime': 'Jan 10, 2011', 'unixTime': 1294646400, 'question': 'How Sturdy? Side to Side or Back and Forth? Does anyone know how sturdy this is?', 'answer': "I'm clumsy and have run into the rocker from both directions (head on and from the side.) We have hardwood floors, and when I've knocked it hard enough to do more than rock, it's just either slid across the floor (without the baby in it) or rocked/stayed in place (with the baby in it.) Obviously, as someone else said, if your bigger boy grabbed on the side to take a look at his sibling, he could possibly exert enough pressure to tip it, but it would take more than a supervising parent would allow, so I wouldn't be as concerned about this risk."}
