{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002QYW8LW', 'answerTime': 'Mar 23, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427094000, 'question': "First Time Mother questions: is this a teether toy or actual first toothbrush? I'm looking for a soft baby toothbrush for her first teeth. TY!", 'answer': "It is a teether. I sent a message to Delta Dental and asked for a child\\toddler brush and they sent me two in the mail. Or ask your dental clinic. The one I have has a special handle that doesn't allow her to choke and the bristles are soft and small. Good luck."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002QYW8LW', 'answerTime': 'Dec 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419840000, 'question': 'At what age do you switch to a "regular" toothbrush? My daughter is 15 months and we still brush with this. Would regular bristles be more effective?', 'answer': 'We started using a regular (baby sized) toothbrush around 12 months. I think it probably depends on whatever you are comfortable with, whether your baby is eating a lot of solid foods, and also what your dentist recommends.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002QYW8LW', 'answerTime': 'Oct 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412233200, 'question': "Is the white part long enough to reach back molars? My 18-month-old is cutting her 2-year molars, and most teethers don't reach that far. Thanks!", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The white part with the brush on the end is not quite 1 1/4" long measuring from the top of the curve on the banana peel. My grandson doesn\'t have molars yet, so I only need to brush his front teeth at this point. I\'m not sure you could easily reach back molars, so you might want to try a different design. I\'m sure I\'ll be buying a child\'s size regular toothbrush when my grandson has all of his teeth. Hope that helps.'}
