{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'May 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399273200, 'question': 'cat?', 'answer': "I don't have cats, but my kids crawled underneath it, which would lead me to think a cat would do the same thing."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Mar 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395730800, 'question': 'It seems some people have installed this at the top of stairs to good effect, but some buyers suggest that this is imprudent. Consensus/thoughts?', 'answer': "We have had ours at the top of the stairs since our son was walking around 10 months he is currently 27 months and we still use it. We have really wide stairs and this was the only gate that anchored to the wall well and didn't seem flimsy. I think if you don't have it installed properly, don't lock the screen, or don't watch then it can be a problem. We always taught our son to not go near the stairs or push on the gate. It has helped to give us a piece of mind if he is playing up stairs, or wakes up in the middle of the night. I don't have to worry about him sleepily falling down the stairs. It has held him too when he's slipped on the floor and couldn't catch himself. Out of a lot of things I have bought for our son, this is one of the top 3 purchases for me!"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 9, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423468800, 'question': 'Does the gate have to be lined up perfectly straight? I need it to stretch across at an angle.', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "It DOESN'T have to be perfectly straight, however, you should line it up with whatever the angel of your entryway/stairs for it to be effective. What I mean is, you can't make it go diagonally across your bottom stair (that's what the instructions say. Mine is slightly askew and it works just fine because there is still solid ground beneath it. I could see a kiddo sneaking under if it were diagonal over a lil open point.) hope that's helpful."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Apr 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1365404400, 'question': 'does this gate make a loud noise when retracting', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "Afraid so, not enough to wake a baby in another room...but you won't be sneaking in or out of the room using this gate."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Aug 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408258800, 'question': 'I want to put this in my kitchen to keep the dog out. How much space is needed to install it? I have 1 3/8 inches between cabinet door and dishwasher.', 'answer': 'You can mount this either where it extends left to right, or right to left. The "roll-up" side requires 2.5 inches; but the hooks on the "latch" side only require 1 inch'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Apr 15, 2015', 'unixTime': 1429081200, 'question': 'Can this be mounted on uneven wall opening, like top of wall is 42", bottom is 38"', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Might be a bit uneven but you could put a spacer to raise it or lower it. Still should work just fine'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Feb 9, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423468800, 'question': 'Does the side the gate retracts into need to be mounted in a wall stud?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "No. I don't have mine in a wall stud and my two year old has yet to break through. Just make sure you affix the screws in with the screw studs."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Jul 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1406012400, 'question': 'Can you operate it with one hand while carrying a baby?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "No, impossible. You need one hand to continually press and turn the locking lever down to release the lock, and the other hand to unhook and support the fabric section as you roll it to open or shut. You can't let go of the gate as you are opening it, otherwise it all flops down, and twists up as it reels in, which will then give you another problem, because if that happens it won't roll out flat with an equal tension top and bottom the next time you try to close it."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002R2BFVM', 'answerTime': 'Sep 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378191600, 'question': "we're looking to install a gate for the bottom of the stairs. One side is the wall and the other side is a metal rod spindle. will this work?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'This gate is a really good option on the stairs and I do recommend it because it\'s out of the way and doesn\'t pose a trip hazard when you don\'t need it. However, as is, you do need to screw the 2 lock clips on the opposite side of the gate into something. Assuming you can\'t screw them into your metal spindle, you could use a kit to connect the gate to the rod. I used the "No Hole Stairway Baby Gate Mounting Kit By Safety Innovations" that I found here on Amazon. It provides a box type clamp around your spindle and a surface to mount (screw) the gate locks into. It wasn\'t particularly easy to install the gate or the kit but now that they are installed, they both work great.'}
