{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002WB2GLQ', 'answerTime': 'Oct 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382425200, 'question': 'It shows that you can swivel the bassinet to either parent facing or forward facing, does the toddler seat have that same feature?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Absolutely. When you change from bassinet to toddler seat it works the same way because it is the same seat. Very convenient. It's a great feature and a good stroller."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GLQ', 'answerTime': 'Jan 18, 2011', 'unixTime': 1295337600, 'question': 'What car seats work with this? How do you know what car seats will work with this?', 'answer': 'According to this link http://mamanetbebe.blogspot.com/2010/02/first-years-wave-stroller.html the First Wave Via car seat and most Chicco and Graco cars seats should work'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GLQ', 'answerTime': 'Apr 19, 2011', 'unixTime': 1303196400, 'question': 'Which buggy board fits best with this stroller?', 'answer': "I bought the sunshine buggy and that seemed to work. Granted it's not a perfect fit and it sways, so you have to be carefull with your drink. Other than that, it works fine."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397631600, 'question': "Will it hold a large toddler comfortably Around 3ft tall and 35lbs? Don't want him cramped like in the Umbrella &amp; feet rubbing the wheels or ground.", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, it will. My son is about 3\'2" and about 40 lbs. and his feet can\'t touch the ground while he\'s sitting in it. They can reach the tops of the wheels, so I have to ask him to keep his feet on the foot rest in the middle, but I definitely find the stroller is big enough for exactly that sized kid. Also, he used be "wrapped up" in the umbrella stroller, like a cocoon. This one has a firmer back with more structure. There\'s room on either side of him while he\'s in the stroller.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Sep 27, 2012', 'unixTime': 1348729200, 'question': 'Connecting 2 Ignite strollers: Hi - Does anyone have experience connecting 2 Ignite strollers side by side using something like the Munchkin link?', 'answer': 'Found this review for the munchkin stroller links: Excellent way to couple two strollers! September 8, 2011 By A. Berke "chemist" We purchased these links to go with our `First Years Ignite\' strollers. They work quite well, with a couple minor annoyances. The links stay on throughout our walks, and though there is play (as other reviewers have mentioned) it turns out to be not too bad with these strollers. I am quite glad that we had two of the same stroller, however, because: 1. The First Years strollers have lockable front wheels, which we appreciated immensely after linking them together. We had to lock the inner two wheels or they would get tangled on turns. There is enough play in the locked wheel to make turning easy, but if we couldn\'t lock the wheels it would have been very frustrating. Make sure to check out the strollers you intend link together to make sure that the front wheels can be locked from turning. 2. There are not many places on the frame to link these two strollers together thanks to the way they bolted the fabric onto the frame. Luckily there are enough spots for a top-middle-bottom link configuration, but I don\'t think I\'d pair one of these strollers with a different brand that had things on the frame in the `wrong\' spots. This review is somewhat intentionally oriented towards the specific strollers we bought and how these links work well (mostly well) with them. In general these links are well-made, sturdy, and *VERY* easy to install/uninstall. They say they fit any stroller, and I believe them... I would just make sure that the stuff not related to the fit on any particular stroller worked first (the wheels and how non-identical strollers match up).'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 7, 2013', 'unixTime': 1365318000, 'question': "Is there a difference in the height of the handles (for taller parents) in the Ignite and the Jet? I'm 6'3 &amp; need a tall stroller???", 'answer': "I've seen a few reviews from others that were 6ft2in and had no problems. So I'm going to assume it wouldn't be Bad for you either."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Mar 31, 2011', 'unixTime': 1301554800, 'question': 'Jet vs ignite??? Has anyone had problems with the wheels being wobbly in the front with the ignite as several people have commented on with the Jet stroller?', 'answer': 'I have both.......we have the pink dot ignite and navy jet......unfortunately I owned the igngite first, so I was spoiled. But the jet is a nice stroller and better than a lot I\'ve seen. The Ignite has much sturdier fabric overall and a more structured back to the seat, there is actually a metal x behind the fabric,(which makes it more flat back like your full size strollers) a inch wider seat base, almost 2 inch taller back of seat, and the locking front wheels for terrain.....it has one parent cup holder and a zippered pouch with mesh pockets, a 5 in variable recline, but its shade does not rotate down, solid plastic grip handles, and 14 lb...I thought it was a great buy at $50 for the convenience of a big stroller in a small stroller. We LOVE IT!!!! The jet\'s fabric feels flimsier, the shade is smaller, but does rotate down, seat is 1 inch smaller, it has 3 very visible vertical back of seat supports without the extra metal x support,(which makes a more rounded back like the umbrella strollers) the front wheels do not lock for terrain and don\'t have as much of a "tread" on them, the parent tray does have 4 spots for drinks with a smaller zippered pouch and mesh pockets, a 2 inch variable recline, foam covered handles, the handles are not as far away from the wheels as the ignite so you may end up kicking it, and 11 lbs.........I wouldn\'t pay more than $35 for it. I t is way better than a small umbrella stroller and even better than the evenflo xsport we have had. Both have the same under storage, locking back wheels, 5 pt harness (but the ignites belt is heavier).......overall the ignite is just made better! We don\'t kick our ignite, but I have kicked they jet a few times.... As long as you don\'t mind the shade not rotating down, I would go with the ignite! Another great buy that makes these stroller even better is a fisher price or safe fit snack pod (depends on where you buy BRU, walmart, target)......it fits perfectly on the side for the kids to have easy access to a snack and drink for $10-12. UPDATE...back from vacation and no problems with either stroller.....wheels were great and both folded easily every time! Except the folding lock on the jet is hard....i didn\'t even bother! Sold the jet and got another ignite! Back from vacation again. Our almost 6 year old, 44in, 43lb son still rides fine in his ignite when needed!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Aug 9, 2013', 'unixTime': 1376031600, 'question': 'What is the minimum age/ or minimum weight of an infant for this stroller?', 'answer': 'I put my granddaughter in it who is 25 inches long, 4 months old and 13 pounds. She slumps to one side. I would think it works better for a 6 month old who has more neck control. It does not lie flat so baby will always be upright or semi-upright. After using it for two weeks, she has already gained more control and a rolled blanket is keeping her more upright. She has no trouble falling asleep in it.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Jan 6, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388995200, 'question': 'Could it fit a 3 year old child? or is it basically for smaller kids? (she does not exceed the weight limit)', 'answer': "It's too hard to determine. Depends on the build of your child. Eg a person 6' tall weighing 180 lbs is slender whereas a person 5' tall weighing 180 is obese. Our 2 yr old granddaughter is petite so she'll fit into it for a long time. If your child is on the chunky side, it's not going to fit as long."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B002WB2GBG', 'answerTime': 'Sep 27, 2012', 'unixTime': 1348729200, 'question': 'What is the best rain cover for the Ignite Stroller? One question: what is a good rain cover to buy for this stroller?', 'answer': "found this review for the Comfy Baby Deluxe Weatherproof Rain Cover with Retractable Sun Shade and Large Easy-Access Window 5.0 out of 5 stars Perfect Fit for my First Years Ignite Stroller!, May 7, 2010 By Candy Coated - See all my reviews Amazon Verified Purchase(What's this?) This review is from: Comfy Baby Deluxe Weatherproof Rain Cover with Retractable Sun Shade and Large Easy-Access Window (Baby Product) It is a perfect fit for my new The First Years Ignite Stroller! The picture of the item here on Amazon does not do justice to the product. The plastic portion in front which opens up via a zipper is very clear, thick and stiff. The zipper area opens quite large so you can tend to your child without removing the entire cover. There are ventilation holes on both side of the cover. The sun shade is another great thing this product offers that other similar stroller covers don't provide. Also, it's nicely folded in a way that it fits flat at the bottom of my stroller basket. It stays there and I pull it out when I need it. This is a great purchase!"}
