{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Nov 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416211200, 'question': "How do you charge this? My daughter is looking for a night light that can't be unplugged for our 2 year old granddaughter.", 'answer': "It works like a cordless phone. There is a base which you place the nightlight on all day, and you simply remove it when you'd like to use it. It is cord free, battery free (in the traditional AA, AAA sense). My daughter has it on a shelf right next to the bed. It really does illuminate the room in a glow. She has also slept with it in the bed, it is completely cool to the touch. You can choose to set it to a short timer or have it light all night long, as well as choose one color or have it color change through its rainbow of choices. They really are adorable."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Oct 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413788400, 'question': 'Can you leave this charging and on all night, or do you have to remove it from the charging station to turn it on as a night light?', 'answer': "I leave it on and on the charger all night, every night. It's lasted 2.5 years so far."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Dec 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419667200, 'question': 'My son wants something he can read with at night without waking his sibling on the lower bunk with the big light. Is this bright enough to read by?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I would say it's absolutely bright enough, but there is no way to direct the light to the page. Being an all-over glow it may cause a problem trying to read with it as I imagine if it was close to him it would shine into his eyes at the same time as on the page, but if it was farther away it may cause eye strain."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Mar 28, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427526000, 'question': 'Is there an easy way to turn it on, in the middle of the night, for diapering and feeding?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'We have the bear and it turns on from the bottom or base of the bear. Not really a simple thing in the middle of the night until you get used to working with it by touch'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Apr 11, 2015', 'unixTime': 1428735600, 'question': 'How long is the battery time if leaving it on?', 'answer': 'Hello Chloe. The KinderGlo will typically last between 8 and 12 hours depending upon several factors. Color mode and battery condition (how well charged the lamp is and the age of the lamp) both affect the use time. Color cycling mode uses slightly more power than a constant color mode, so the light will last for slightly less time on this setting. We hope this is helpful!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Apr 3, 2015', 'unixTime': 1428044400, 'question': "Is there a way to reset the night light? The Dino stopped working last night. No red light when charging doesn't turn on.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "If you see no light at all it could be a problem with the base, not the Dino part. We had a problem with the base prong once. I emailed the Kinderglo company and they trouble- shooted with me to try to determine if it was something easily fixable. When it wasn't they asked me for my information and sent me a new base. In my case the metal prong was bend a bit and it simply wouldn't line up correctly to form a connection."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Feb 19, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424332800, 'question': "I'm looking for a red night light. Do you have to cycle through the different color settings every time you turn it on?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0036Z3DDC', 'answerTime': 'Feb 7, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423296000, 'question': "Blue light is bad to use because it stunts melatonin. Is there an option of yellow light? Can't imagine red wd be soothing.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Hello Ninjabetic, and thanks for your question. At this time, a solid yellow setting is not available for the KinderGlo. The four light settings are solid green, solid blue, solid red, and continuous color changing. While some of the colors in the cycle are brighter than others, in general all of the light settings are soft enough to sleep by. As far as concerns about impeding melatonin levels, the science behind this shows that lights with a brightness of under 5 lux have no effect on the body's production of melatonin. Because we use soft LED lights which are then filtered through a translucent plastic body, the brightness of the KinderGlo never reaches 1 lux. The lights are bright enough to see by, but soft enough to sleep by."}
