{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Sep 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411110000, 'question': 'Will this gate fit a 33 1/2" opening (almost 34") and a 35 1/2" opening? Thanks so much for your help!', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'The instruction sheet states that without extensions it will fit 28"-31.5". With one extension it will fit 31.5"-35.5". With two extensions it will fit 35.5"-38.5". My opening was just over 33" so I had to use the gate with one extension. Hope that helps.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Aug 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1377414000, 'question': "I've seen some reviews that say the walk-thru is small. How wide is the walk-thru?", 'answer': 'Walk thru is 17 inches wide. Easy enough for a person, but if you want to take something wider thru the gate you may have to take the gate down to do so. But I have managed to take a storage crate thru by turning it on its side.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Oct 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412233200, 'question': 'Are the hinges plastic or metal?', 'answer': "There's a piece of something--can't tell whether it's hard plastic or metal--that is permanently affixed to a metal piece. The hinging action comes from a hidden piece (so I certainly can't tell what that's made of) that pivots inside the piece described above. The piece described in sentence one does not move. It seems solid and substantial, but I can say that the piece I know to be metal has a different sound when I tap on it than the piece that's permanently affixed and does not move. Clear as mud? Don't picture something like a door hinge--it's altogether different."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'May 26, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401087600, 'question': "can this gate be installed so it's rounded out like in the video clip?", 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'No, the "North States Supergate Extra Tall Easy Close Gate, Bronze" can not be "rounded" like in the video. You have to get the "Supergate Deluxe Decor Gate" in order to get the "rounded" version of the gate system.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Jul 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405494000, 'question': 'If our doorway is 29" wide will this fit? The ad is confusing: "28" 31.5" wide". Is it 28 or 31.5 at it\'s narrowest?', 'answer': 'There are manual adjustments that screw on the side and also additional panels included with the gate. Yes it will definitely fit.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Aug 25, 2013', 'unixTime': 1377414000, 'question': 'Is there anything under the bottom rail that would protect the floor from being scratched?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "No, but you can put a piece of material under it or install it raised off the floor a fraction of an inch. You really don't have to worry about it marring your floor if it's installed properly, the main section doesn't move, the gate swings, but there's a small bar under it that doesn't move."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0038OLMXE', 'answerTime': 'Apr 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398841200, 'question': 'Does it fit in a sliding glass door?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "While it may 'fit' in the opening, it will not be secure enough with the tension resting on the sliding door (the door on the one side will not be wide enough to hold the tension 'rings' on the end of the screws). I would not advise using this for a sliding door. They do make models that do fit sliding doors though - http://www.amazon.com/Cardinal-Gates-Lock-n-Block-Sliding-24-Inches/dp/B003TFEF3G/ref=sr_1_2?ie=UTF8&qid=1398872451&sr=8-2&keywords=Cardinal+Gates+Lock-N-Block+Sliding+Door+Gate - try that link."}
