{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0039ONKFQ', 'answerTime': 'Mar 13, 2014', 'unixTime': 1394694000, 'question': 'Does this product prevent someone (a burglar) from using a crow bar to lift out the sliding door?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'In my opinion YES, it will prevent that. It is double bolted and secured to the side framing of the door. With a past in public safety, I researched this quite a bit because in our new home we can not use a simple pin to secure the door over and above the cheap latches that come on sliding doors. When I researched this type of lock and passed it around a few police friends we were all in agreement that this would ultimately keep the door attached and unable to be removed from the tracks as normal access with a crow bar or heavy screw driver would allow. I highly recommend it.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0039ONKFQ', 'answerTime': 'Feb 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391500800, 'question': 'The description says no key but I see a small key? Does anyone have this "new" feature? Do you have to use the key everytime you want to lock the door', 'answer': "Hello, The key is a extra feature on new lock for Child Safety, and if you don't use door a lot or gone along time you might want to lock. Stick key in slot of hole and slight click will lock so kids or Burglars cant open ! You don't have to use , it is a extra feature."}
