{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJHDQW', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424937600, 'question': "Are these very bulky when pad-folded? Right now, I'm using 4x6 diapers, which are very trim, but I need more and these are cheaper.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'These are not really bulky. Not the thinest ones but not too bulky. I would say go for it :) Although I would recommend more these ones http://www.amazon.com/OsoCozy-Pack-Birdseye-Unbleached-Diapers/dp/B004GTLRY4/ref=sr_1_4?s=baby-products&ie=UTF8&qid=1424973111&sr=1-4 The best from all different ones me and my husband tried. Really great.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Dec 24, 2013', 'unixTime': 1387872000, 'question': 'Just switched from the size 1 for my 4 month old who is 15 lbs, but they are huge! Suggestion for how you made them fit?', 'answer': 'I think you are supposed to move to size 2 at 18 lbs. Hope that helps! :) I am still using size 1 on my 15.5 lb baby (who is 8 months old, lol!)'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Aug 8, 2013', 'unixTime': 1375945200, 'question': "I'm wondering the average size of the size 2's after shrinking...I have an up and coming chubbers! Could anyone share measurements after washing?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'I wash on hot, dry on sanitize (extra hot and a bit longer than the normal cycle) and mine measure 17 3/4" long, 12 1/4" wide. My son is 31lbs and the waist is getting a little tight to snappi easily, but other than that, they fit great. I\'ve found tutorials online as to add width to the waist, so that\'s what we\'ll be doing.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': '12 days ago', 'question': 'Is there a larger size than size 2', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'There is a toddler size that is bigger,30-45 lbs, but it is only available by the dozen'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Dec 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1418976000, 'question': 'Are there tags on these? Oddly, to my 2 year old, a very important part of the burp cloth turned "binkie-blankie." Thanks! :)', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "No tags on these (but omg have you seen the taggie blankets that have the twenty or so tags all around...). We have some other cloth diapers we bought online from http://www.greenmountaindiapers.com/cloth-diapers.html. Those ones have tags and were way better quality than these OsoCozy ones. But if you are using for burp cloths probably doesn't matter."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Oct 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1412319600, 'question': 'How many layers are each of the two sizes?', 'answer': 'The sizes actually differ in lengths, both of which fold like a piece of paper fitted into an envelope. The back half is unfolded and open while the front half is generally folded. Luckily the package has a picture diagram with explanations. It does seem overwhelming at first, but you will quickly get the hang of it. I find that I I fold the two side flaps over the center after washing that they are readily handy for use. Good luck!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Apr 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397113200, 'question': 'Do you need to get diaper pins for these or are there snaps in them?', 'answer': "Yes you need to get something to hold the diaper together after you've folded it onto the baby. I LOVE the Snappi fasteners, they come in a pack of three and they are awesome. Haven't had any issue at all with them coming undone. I also use a Thristies Duo Wrap Snap Cover."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJHDR6', 'answerTime': 'Aug 29, 2014', 'unixTime': 1409295600, 'question': "Has anyone found that the Size 2 diaper isn't wide enough? My little guy is only 18 pounds but it seems to be a really tight fit width-wise w/ Snappis", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'These started getting tight on my daughter when she was about 18 pounds as well. I suggest checking out the medium wide cloth-eez prefold diapers on www.greenmountaindiapers.com.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Dec 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386835200, 'question': 'Read reviews still super confused. What all do I need to buy with this to start being able to use?', 'answer': 'This is just the waterproof shell that goes on the outside of the diaper. You will need the absorbent insides. You have several options here: 1- you can use an old school flat diaper which you fold and pin around your baby. If you use those, you will need "flats" and something to pin them with, either pins or snappis. 2- you can use what is called a fitted diaper. Those are less intimidating than flats. They look similar to the outer shell but are soft and absorbent. They sometimes have snaps on them to close them and sometimes not. If they are snapless, you may want a pin or a snappi. 3- you can use prefolds which are really similar to flats but are already sewn into a partially folded shape. You may want a snappi or pin for these too. 4- you can simply lay something absorbent into this shell without pinning or snapping it around your baby. You can use a folded up flat or prefold for this or a cloth diaper insert (NOT the microfiber kind because those cannot go next to baby\'s skin, but you could use hemp, cotton, or bamboo). In this case you would not need pins or snappis. I don\'t like this as much because it doesn\'t contain poop as well and so poop can get on the cover which means you have to use a new cover. If the cover doesn\'t get poop on it, you can reuse it. Hope that helps!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Feb 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392451200, 'question': "I purchased this cover and some thirsties duo inserts. They don't seem to go together. Does the duo insert just lay in the cover? No way to secure it", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "It just lies inside the cover. The cover can be used over prefolds or with Flip inserts. I use it with Nickis prefold in large and it's very good. My LO is 7.5 months and it just fits her well. She's 18lbs. I think the duo inserts can be used for them."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Jun 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402383600, 'question': 'How many of these will I need to get me started. For example, how many will I need to get through 1 day with a newborn? Thank you !', 'answer': "I followed my friend's advice. She had twins and used these. start with 6, you will have more than enough. You don't always need to put them to wash every day, if the poop has not touched the wrap, you can reuse it. However, we did not use it for our newborn, she was too small, 6.3lbs. We started with 6, we are still using them and our daughter is now 6 moths old. They are great."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Jan 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1390809600, 'question': 'Is this product machine washable?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes! It's very durable too. I let mine hang dry and they look brand new after 9 months."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382511600, 'question': 'How does this diaper work? Is it like Econobum or Flip, where you put a folded prefold/insert flat in, or diaper in a prefold and cover?', 'answer': "It's just like the Econobum or Flip covers - you can use either a prefold or an insert inside of it. We found that the Thirsties covers were really great for when our baby was very small, because they have extra coverage around the leg holes. But, he's already grown out of them (by 7 months)."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'May 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399878000, 'question': 'How many covers are needed if you want to do laundry every other day?', 'answer': 'Bare minimum of 5, but newborn poo is explosive, runny, and frequent, so it is very possible that you could need more. If the elastic or gussets get wet, or if poop gets into the seams, they need to be washed, otherwise they can be wiped and reused.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Jan 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388563200, 'question': "We're TTC #2 and I'm seriously considering cloth diapering this time around. Any known leakage issues or latex bands with this set?", 'answer': "I don't know about the latex but we have had virtually no leakage issues with this wrap when we fold our prefold cloth insert properly. It took us awhile to figure out what worked for our baby, I watched a video on YouTube on how to fold the prefold different ways to minimize leaks and immediately saw improvement when coupled with this wrap. We also used the snappis for awhile while we were getting the hang of cloth diapering, this also seemed to help stop leaks."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Dec 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419753600, 'question': 'Has anyone tried these with a newborn?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, I have used these with a newborn. The prefolds I used were large enough for a 1+ year old, so the diaper as a whole was quite large. But the wrap worked as expected.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Feb 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391587200, 'question': 'Can these covers be used while the cord stump is still healing? By folding down the top or something?', 'answer': 'I\'m not totally sure because we used disposables for the first week or two. However, in general, they fit below the belly button. I\'ve heard it described as "bikini cut" when you use with trifolds.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY1K', 'answerTime': 'Feb 5, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423123200, 'question': "I've seen this same diaper with either 3 or 2 snaps in the front. Does this one have 2 or 3 snaps? Any pros to snaps vs velcro?", 'answer': "There are two snaps on each side. But they are only in a single row. So they go horizontally they are not vertical. There are rise snap so you can adjust for a smaller or larger babies and there's two settings but three snaps vertically. As to the Velcro versus snaps: for ease of use I always find myself grabbing Velcro first. It just is so much easier to get the perfect fit, and it's just easy. Except you do have to remember to use the laundry tabs when they get yucky so that means when they have poop on them and you want to just put it in your diaper bin you have to remember to fold the tabs and which kind of sucks, and my husband always forgets. Another downside to the Velcro is they really will not last as long as snaps. I have not looked into it but there's a lot of videos on how to supposedly, easily, repair the Velcro. I have repaired elastic on my diapers and it's pretty straightforward so that should be similar. That being said I prefer to buy snaps, with the hopes that they will last longer. On some of our diapers I've noticed even after only two months of use, and using the laundry tabs, that the Velcro just isn't the same. that worries me. With my first child we got by with only snaps and it was just fine. Hope that helps!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Dec 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386835200, 'question': 'Read reviews still super confused. What all do I need to buy with this to start being able to use?', 'answer': 'This is just the waterproof shell that goes on the outside of the diaper. You will need the absorbent insides. You have several options here: 1- you can use an old school flat diaper which you fold and pin around your baby. If you use those, you will need "flats" and something to pin them with, either pins or snappis. 2- you can use what is called a fitted diaper. Those are less intimidating than flats. They look similar to the outer shell but are soft and absorbent. They sometimes have snaps on them to close them and sometimes not. If they are snapless, you may want a pin or a snappi. 3- you can use prefolds which are really similar to flats but are already sewn into a partially folded shape. You may want a snappi or pin for these too. 4- you can simply lay something absorbent into this shell without pinning or snapping it around your baby. You can use a folded up flat or prefold for this or a cloth diaper insert (NOT the microfiber kind because those cannot go next to baby\'s skin, but you could use hemp, cotton, or bamboo). In this case you would not need pins or snappis. I don\'t like this as much because it doesn\'t contain poop as well and so poop can get on the cover which means you have to use a new cover. If the cover doesn\'t get poop on it, you can reuse it. Hope that helps!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Feb 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392451200, 'question': "I purchased this cover and some thirsties duo inserts. They don't seem to go together. Does the duo insert just lay in the cover? No way to secure it", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "It just lies inside the cover. The cover can be used over prefolds or with Flip inserts. I use it with Nickis prefold in large and it's very good. My LO is 7.5 months and it just fits her well. She's 18lbs. I think the duo inserts can be used for them."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Jun 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402383600, 'question': 'How many of these will I need to get me started. For example, how many will I need to get through 1 day with a newborn? Thank you !', 'answer': "I followed my friend's advice. She had twins and used these. start with 6, you will have more than enough. You don't always need to put them to wash every day, if the poop has not touched the wrap, you can reuse it. However, we did not use it for our newborn, she was too small, 6.3lbs. We started with 6, we are still using them and our daughter is now 6 moths old. They are great."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Jan 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1390809600, 'question': 'Is this product machine washable?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes! It's very durable too. I let mine hang dry and they look brand new after 9 months."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Oct 23, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382511600, 'question': 'How does this diaper work? Is it like Econobum or Flip, where you put a folded prefold/insert flat in, or diaper in a prefold and cover?', 'answer': "It's just like the Econobum or Flip covers - you can use either a prefold or an insert inside of it. We found that the Thirsties covers were really great for when our baby was very small, because they have extra coverage around the leg holes. But, he's already grown out of them (by 7 months)."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'May 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1399878000, 'question': 'How many covers are needed if you want to do laundry every other day?', 'answer': 'Bare minimum of 5, but newborn poo is explosive, runny, and frequent, so it is very possible that you could need more. If the elastic or gussets get wet, or if poop gets into the seams, they need to be washed, otherwise they can be wiped and reused.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Jan 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388563200, 'question': "We're TTC #2 and I'm seriously considering cloth diapering this time around. Any known leakage issues or latex bands with this set?", 'answer': "I don't know about the latex but we have had virtually no leakage issues with this wrap when we fold our prefold cloth insert properly. It took us awhile to figure out what worked for our baby, I watched a video on YouTube on how to fold the prefold different ways to minimize leaks and immediately saw improvement when coupled with this wrap. We also used the snappis for awhile while we were getting the hang of cloth diapering, this also seemed to help stop leaks."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Dec 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419753600, 'question': 'Has anyone tried these with a newborn?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, I have used these with a newborn. The prefolds I used were large enough for a 1+ year old, so the diaper as a whole was quite large. But the wrap worked as expected.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003AJXY3I', 'answerTime': 'Feb 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391587200, 'question': 'Can these covers be used while the cord stump is still healing? By folding down the top or something?', 'answer': 'I\'m not totally sure because we used disposables for the first week or two. However, in general, they fit below the belly button. I\'ve heard it described as "bikini cut" when you use with trifolds.'}
