{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003CL8ICQ', 'answerTime': 'Jul 17, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405580400, 'question': "i cannot get this to work! the diaphragms don't expand after contracting and i get NO suction. please help!!", 'answer': "So they just contract and stay contracted? Has the pump worked for you before or is this the first time you're using it? First, I'd make sure all the parts are connected properly (push firmly at each connection), especially the white valves underneath the flanges. If that doesn't help, inspect each part of the kit (valves, diaphragm, tubing) for any tears or holes. Do you have any spare parts you can swap out to see if it's one of your parts? If none of the above work, try using the manual pump with the kit for now and call Customer Service. It may be your motor. :( I had problems with suction a few times but it was always the result of forgetting to properly assemble some part, probably because of sleep deprivation. Hopefully it starts working for you soon!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003CL8ICQ', 'answerTime': 'Jun 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1402729200, 'question': 'When first purchased, is the hygienikit marked "sterile" or must it be cleaned prior to use? Also, for those who have used the pump for a while, would you recommend purchasing a spare hygienikit (how durable are the valves, etc.)?', 'answer': 'Mine was not marked "sterile," so I boiled all the parts the user guide indicated I should. I would absolutely buy extra valves, tubing, and plastic diaphragms for the pump, as it won\'t work at all if something goes wrong with the ones you have (I.e. they are punctured somehow or get a small tear). That being said, I\'ve been using the original set that came with my pump since I bought it (7 months ago) without problems, so the advice is just a precaution.'}
