{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003IP8RRM', 'answerTime': 'Jan 31, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391155200, 'question': "How can I remove these? They broke but now I can't get them off of my cabinets. Help please!", 'answer': "I put the handle end of one of the gerber metal infant feeding spoons down between the cabinet and strap and very gently worked it off. Find anything that's strong and can be worked down in between- just be careful not to pry it off because you could damage your furniture. Mine came off with no residue left behind. Good luck!!"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003IP8RRM', 'answerTime': 'Jan 9, 2014', 'unixTime': 1389254400, 'question': 'Would these work if I were to use them on my oven?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "We have the safety first front oven lock as well as this one too on our oven. (Double oven). Both have been working really well. These ones however for cabinets we've had to replace here and there but I do really like this product. Especially if your cabinets are like ours where we couldn't put in a more permanent safety hook/lock. The stove use was a bonus. We had to pull the stove out slightly so we could stick one side on the oven door and the other to the side of the oven. You cut the connecting piece to size."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003ITAUTG', 'answerTime': 'Nov 20, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384934400, 'question': 'How many years is the Recaro good for, when does it expire?', 'answer': "6 years from manufacturing. Mine was made in the same month as ordered (both November 2013), so it's good until 2019."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003ITAUTG', 'answerTime': 'Sep 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378191600, 'question': 'How is the ProRide on an airplane?', 'answer': "We have used it on 8 flights so far. It is fine in the sense that it fits on the plane (we had it rear-facing) and it works the same as it would in a car. The downside is the weight of this carseat means it is not easy to haul this carseat onto the plane and through airports between flights (we strapped it to a cart to go from one gate to another). The other downside is that the person who sits in front of you won't be able to recline their seat on the plane at all and this may upset them (but this is probably true for most carseats). I would take it on a plane again."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003ITAUTG', 'answerTime': 'Oct 28, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382943600, 'question': 'Will this fit, rear-facing, in a 2003 Toyota Corolla?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'It fits rear-facing in the back of our 2004 Honda Civic and 2005 Toyota Matrix. As mentioned in my review of the seat, in the Civic, we had to put it behind the driver\'s seat because when behind the passenger seat it blocks sight out of that window (which is needed for a lot of highway driving). The windows in the Matrix are of a size that the window isn\'t completely blocked, so the seat can go behind the passenger seat easily. I am 5\'7" and have enough room with the seat installed behind the driver\'s seat; at 6\'1" my husband has barely enough room, but it\'s doable for short distances. So while I am not sure of the dimensions of the rear seat of a Corolla, I\'m not imagining that it is a whole lot smaller than a Civic. It should fit fine if you install it behind the passenger seat, that is, if you have a large-ish back passenger window or if seeing out of it is not a concern for you. If you are tall and have to put it behind the driver\'s seat, this may be a problem. (Note that once forward-facing, it won\'t block the window and can go behind the passenger seat easily since it also takes up less room in that position). It really isn\'t that much larger than many other good seats on the market. Having a small car is a challenge with a lot of seats. So while neither of our cars has a lot of room left over after installing the seat, it does fit. Hope this helps without directly answering your question.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B003ITAUTG', 'answerTime': 'Feb 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393488000, 'question': "Does this seat's fabric provide good air flow? My current britax makes my son sweat a lot.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I live in southwest Texas and so far no problems! Once your child is bigger and you can remove the padded seat liner the seat will be even cooler. The only time my little guy gets sweaty is on his back if we are in the car for a long stretch, but other than that we don't have any problems and in my opinion the fabric and seat design has better air flow than the Britax."}
