{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003LPUQOG', 'answerTime': 'Jul 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404198000, 'question': 'Are these for pocket diapers, or can they be used as the main insert in hybrids?', 'answer': "These are made to use with just a cover. Like flip cover or thirsties duo covers. You could use then with a pocket diaper but it would be very bulky. They are made to be able to touch the baby's skin."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003LPUQOG', 'answerTime': 'Apr 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396681200, 'question': "How do you know which side of the insert goes against baby's skin?", 'answer': "The good news with these is either since the microfiber is tucked inside and the snaps when together have only the safe fabrics outside against the baby's skin."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003LPUQOG', 'answerTime': 'Mar 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395385200, 'question': 'Is the hemp bottom layer the same hemp pad that you can buy alone? Can you can use the hemp alone in a pocket diaper?', 'answer': "I don't think you can buy them standalone. I actually have hemp inserts that I got from BabyKicks JoeyBunz and I prefer them for a stand alone doubler. The thirsties version can be unsnapped from the microfleece layer, but it does have snaps on it, so you'd want to only turn the soft side toward the baby if you do decide to use it without the fleece layer. I like the thirsties inserts for absorbancy in a quick trip out and about, but if your kid #2s in their diaper, it's a bit of a mess because it gets all over the cover!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003LPUQOG', 'answerTime': 'Mar 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1394434800, 'question': "Do you use these by themselves with the covers or do you have to use them with another type of insert? Don't know much about the new styles. Haven't used cloth diapers since my little sisters 15+ years ago. Thanks!", 'answer': 'You can go to the duo website for more info... But you need a cover and then one of these. Depending on how much your little one pees you may need to double up with a trifold-I did. By about 17 lbs we had outgrown this size and ended up switching to 7th generation disposable diapers.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003LPUQOG', 'answerTime': 'May 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401260400, 'question': 'I really want to use cloth diapers when I have my baby but, I need to know how many of these inserts should I purchase to get started?', 'answer': "I'd recommend a minimum of 12 - if you do Landry everyday. I have the Thirsties prefolds as well and absolutely love them. I'm still using the small size on my petite 18 month old. I had 1 of each and just liked them so much better than these. Better investment and useful longer, as well as more versatile. But maybe get a few of each and see which you like best. You can always add more of what you like to your stash and with a newborn, you almost can't have too many inserts. Definitely hemp is the way to go. Much more absorbent than other fabrics and most are blended with cotton so there is no need to top it for improved absorption."}
