{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003TRZIGW', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424937600, 'question': 'from what age babies?', 'answer': 'The more important thing is the stage your child is at. Can they sit up on their own, and are they under the recommended weight limit per seat (I think it was 35 pounds but not 100% sure)? My twin boys started using this at about 5.5 months. So it is more making sure your child is ready and about to sit in a regular seat (though it can recline as well); they should be able to support their own head/neck and sit without much help before this.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003TU83SE', 'answerTime': 'Nov 13, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384329600, 'question': 'Formamide levels? Does anyone know how these mats fare in terms of Formamide levels?', 'answer': 'A lot of customers were concern after France and Belgium issued a ban on EVA foam mats. However, these two countries have reversed the ban and reduced the restriction limit to &gt;200 mg/kg on how much formamide foam mats can contain instead. Keep in mind that no other countries have banned or set restrictions on formamide. http://newsletter.sgs.com/eNewsletterPro/uploadedimages/000006/sgs-safeguards-1291212-france-extends-temporary-ban-for-formamide-in-puzzle-mats-a4-en-12.pdf Australia conducted their own test on formamide in foam mats and have found no health risk, you can read the article in the following link: http://www.productsafety.gov.au/content/index.phtml/itemId/989496 All our mats have pass and exceed all US regulations. Our standard mats have not been specifically tested for formamide as we are not required nor any real tests show that it is a safety concern to children. According to our manufacturer in Taiwan, our standard mats meet the formamide limit set by France at &gt;100 mg/kg. In order to better serve our concerned customers, we have brought in formamide non-detectable mats. You can find the reports in the following link: http://www.ewonderworld.com/test%20report/ACT-Mats_FormamideNDTestReport.pdf You can order the Formamide Non-Detectable mats in the following link: http://www.amazon.com/gp/product/B008YGT2GK Please note we do NOT plan on carrying these formamide ND mats in the future so supply is limited. It is too costly(to manufacture and for our customers as well), low demand, have not been proven to be a health risk to children nor is required by country other than France and Belgium. Let me know if you have any more questions regarding formamide or foam mats in general.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B003TU83SE', 'answerTime': 'Mar 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395385200, 'question': 'Also which side up? -- I purchased one set and just received it. I noticed that one side is very smooth, the other side is rough.', 'answer': 'I put the rough side up because I think it wears better. It also helps grip little feet. The smooth side can be almost slippery, especially on stocking feet, but stays put on the floor.'}
