{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0043G8OXC', 'answerTime': 'Feb 19, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424332800, 'question': "I'm looking for a red night light. Do you have to cycle through the different color settings every time you turn it on?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0043G8OXC', 'answerTime': 'Feb 7, 2015', 'unixTime': 1423296000, 'question': "Blue light is bad to use because it stunts melatonin. Is there an option of yellow light? Can't imagine red wd be soothing.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "Hello Ninjabetic, and thanks for your question. At this time, a solid yellow setting is not available for the KinderGlo. The four light settings are solid green, solid blue, solid red, and continuous color changing. While some of the colors in the cycle are brighter than others, in general all of the light settings are soft enough to sleep by. As far as concerns about impeding melatonin levels, the science behind this shows that lights with a brightness of under 5 lux have no effect on the body's production of melatonin. Because we use soft LED lights which are then filtered through a translucent plastic body, the brightness of the KinderGlo never reaches 1 lux. The lights are bright enough to see by, but soft enough to sleep by."}
