{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y30', 'answerTime': 'Mar 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1362297600, 'question': "I currently have a chevy traverse and am looking at getting a suburban. Does anyone have these kinds of cars where the booster seat didn't work?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Hi Tia. I am an automotive consultant and as far as I am aware, this seat should work fine with either of the vehicles you mentioned. I know of two that are used in midsize sedans (Camry, CTS) and another that is in a Toyota Venza and they all work with no problem at all. This seat does have the LATCH connections at the base, but they are used solely to keep the seat in place when a child is NOT sitting in it. When a child is in it, the seatbelt keeps both the child and booster seat in place. I have this seat personally in my own car and it has been working well for the past couple of months. I hope this helps and feel free to contact www.autoacademics.net if you have any other questions. Chris, Automotive Advisor and Consultant'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y30', 'answerTime': 'Jan 21, 2013', 'unixTime': 1358755200, 'question': 'Does the back come off so this can be a backless booster?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It does - it arrives with the back off in the box and instructions on how to attach/detach it (very easy).'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y30', 'answerTime': 'Jan 21, 2013', 'unixTime': 1358755200, 'question': 'Can you remove the cover to clean it?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes. It has little elastic loops that attach to little hooks on the seat part and on the back part. The back is completely removable as well. Super easy to get in and out of the car. Very light weight. Hands down the best booster seat.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y2G', 'answerTime': 'Sep 19, 2013', 'unixTime': 1379574000, 'question': 'My 7 year-old is 51 inches tall. Will this Britax Parkway high-backed booster seat will accommodate a child who is that tall?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Hi. My 5 year old is not quite that big yet, but the back extends quite high. The height of the back extended is about 28 inches from the top of the car seat to the top of the back. Also, the back can be removed and seat used as a booster. In fact, the booster is also highly rated.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y2G', 'answerTime': 'Oct 19, 2012', 'unixTime': 1350630000, 'question': "Several reviewers complained about the seat belt guide piece - sounds like if the child leans forward &amp; the belt pulls out, it doesn't always retract? Anyone else having this issue?", 'answer': "I didn't have a problem with the belt pulling out. I had an issue with the entire seat back coming forward when I braked. I only used it for one trip down the road and after I saw that happen, I took it out and returned it. Interestingly, the clerk at the store where it was purchased was not surprised that we weren't happy with it and said he sees a lot of them returned. Hope that helps!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B0047O3Y2G', 'answerTime': 'Aug 24, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408863600, 'question': "I have a Volvo XC90 . When I installed the Parkway, the forward slope of the headrest wouldn't allow the seat to lean back properly for install?", 'answer': "I don't know the year of your Volvo XC90 but I was just looking at pictures of a 2015. It looks like the headrest is adjustable to go back to a more straight up and more flat position. It also looks like the headrest was removable. Take a look at the headrest on yours to see if any of this works for yours. Hope that this helps!!"}
