{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004AMVVJI', 'answerTime': 'Jan 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388908800, 'question': 'can you measure the room temperature or bathtub water temperature?', 'answer': "The thermometer has 2 modes... for reading people's temperature and for reading the temperature of objects. The second mode can be used for reading the temperature of a room (point at the wall) or a bathtub (or other pot of water, I have used it for measuring milk temperature making cheese). Note that you have to switch modes, which takes fumbling through some poorly designed menus, so it is not a fast process. However, it can be used for general measuring of objects within normal temperature ranges (min is 32* F, max I have not found exactly but it can measure at least 110* F)."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004AMVVJI', 'answerTime': 'Jan 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388908800, 'question': 'does this unit work on dogs?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'Very unlikely, although it would depend on the dog. The unit is easily confused by hair. It also requires a good shot at the forehead or another spot that leaks heat easily. Inside the mouth generally does not work well. Around the eyes can work, but often the eyeball itself reflects too much IR for an accurate reading. To take a reading from a dog, you would need either a dog with a large forehead and very little hair (perhaps a weimaraner, but I wouldn\'t count on it) or you would need to take a reading from inside the ear. This might be accurate (the ear canal would "leak heat" due to high blood flow and has little hair) but I wouldn\'t count on it.'}
