{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004CLBX2M', 'answerTime': 'Feb 28, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393574400, 'question': 'I have two openings that I need a gate for, one is 49" and the other 51 1/2". Does anyone know if this gate will work?', 'answer': 'Kacie, just off the top of my head, I really don\'t know or know if they have come out with wider gates. My opening is 34 3/4" with one extension. If you put a extension on both sides, maybe or if you added 2x4\'s on each side to pick up some slack you might be able to get the gate to work, but then you reach a point where the gate becomes a little weak, the whole gate. The better you can use the gate with out to much add ons (extensions) the more sturdy and stronger the gate. It is a great gate, maybe the mfg has made wider gates, would hurt to ask, like I say it is a great doggie gate and would work great for a child gate. bryan'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004CLBX2M', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393401600, 'question': 'My gate door does not reach the latch thus I cannot lock the gate and it just swings open. Is there a piece that extends the latch or something? Its about 3 inch gap between the latch and door latch.', 'answer': 'This gate requires pressure applied to the opposing walls to make the top squeeze together. Read the instructions on how to tighten it up.'}
