{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004DC9TF8', 'answerTime': 'May 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1400137200, 'question': 'I have a tall skinny 5 year old and a newborn. My 5 year old is 46in and only 41lbs. Do you think he would fit ok?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'I have 4 kids and my youngest three can interchange in the stroller. My 7 mo is always in there and then my 3 and 6 yo sometimes take turns. My 6 yo is a girl and she is a little tall and stocky for her age. She can ride in the stroller. Her head is a little cramped up under the hood, but seat wise she is fine. It does make the stroller noticeably harder to navigate when she is in it because of the weight increase. She is around 65 lbs. Good luck deciding there are so many variables ;) It is a really great stroller!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004DC9TF8', 'answerTime': 'Feb 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391414400, 'question': "I'm trying to decide which double bob to order. I have a revolution but live in an area with brick streets. Run 15-20 miles a week.", 'answer': "I own both the Ironman Dualie and the Revolution Dualie (and coach cross country) and have found that the revolution is more run worthy than I thought it would be. I fun with the front wheel free, as the fixed wheel doesn't hold a line well, and the free wheel is so easy to steer, particularly with the heavier load. The only scenarios where I have minor issues are on routes with meaningful hills, as the revolution has no hand brake, and when running faster than 8 minute miles over bumps. If the front end comes off the ground at faster speeds the wheel drifts and when it comes down catches momentarily before straightening out, which causes a sudden deceleration. Not an issue at jogging speeds, but something to watch for. That said I trained for my first marathon with the Revolution. The Ironman is awesome for trails/off-road that's stroller worthy, hills, and for running on paved trails. I ran our neighborhood with it for two years and you adapt to steering with the front wheel up pretty quickly. In either case, keep track of your load weight and adjust the shock setting when you exceed 50 lbs."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004DC9TF8', 'answerTime': 'Mar 20, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395298800, 'question': 'Is this a good stroller for gravel roads?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes, it is a good running stroller. It would most likely be one of the better ones for off roads too, due to thick tires and shock system. It just isn't a good stroller for something like a stroll at the zoo because it is difficult to turn."}
