{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004VL2VRO', 'answerTime': 'Sep 5, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378364400, 'question': 'does this model have the capability to turn the screen off while keeping the audio on?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes. Here\'s how the screen function works: 1. When not connected with the AC power adapter (solely on battery power) the screen will automatically time out after approximately 2 min. Audio will remain "on". You can keep the screen from turning off by pressing the screen button (lower right side, second from the bottom) which will keep the screen on but obviously use more power. 2. When connected to the AC power adapter simply press the screen button to toggle the screen on & off. Otherwise the default setting with the power adapter connected is the screen on. The audio will remain on and is adjustable via the onscreen menu. Hope this helps!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004VL2VRO', 'answerTime': 'Aug 29, 2012', 'unixTime': 1346223600, 'question': 'Wireless range extension question: Can this unit be set up to operate over a home wireless system (ie - go through my router) to extend the range?', 'answer': "I don't believe this thing uses an IP network. It's more like a wireless phone. I'd be surprised if it wasn't just a DECT (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_Enhanced_Cordless_Telecommunications) device. The only way I could imagine getting this thing to integrate with a LAN would be to connect the Motorola A/V cable from your parent unit into a video capture card, and stream the result via your network. That's a *lot* more work than it'd be worth, IMO. You may want to just get yourself something like an x10 Airsight, or something. Like this: Genuine Foscam Fi8908w Wireless Ip Camera Network with Pan & Tilt,night Vision,2 Way Audio,black"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004VL2VS8', 'answerTime': 'Jul 15, 2012', 'unixTime': 1342335600, 'question': 'Trouble with Night Vision (Motorola MBP20 Baby Monitor) Does the monitor require pitch dark conditions to work?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The unit doesn't simply see into darkness: it still has to have some kind of light reflected back to it in order to see anything. That's where those little dots around the camera lens come in. They're infrared lamps, and they're really nothing more than little flashlights, except they're sending out a wavelength of light we can't see with the naked eye. Take the best, most expensive night-vision goggles used by any of the top military units in the world, and they won't see a thing in the dark without their accompanying infrared lamps. Take another look at the lamps around the lens on the baby unit - they're incredibly tiny and not very powerful (the MBP20 is a pretty bare-bones model, after all, despite all the touted features). Picture a very tiny, weak, regular flashlight and imagine the feeble light THAT would put out in a dark room, and you'll understand why Motorola had to put four of the IR lamps around the lens instead of just one. So that's the science. Now for the actual suggestions. Low-tech and cheap: Find a way to move the camera closer to where the action is. If you're trying to use the night vision feature then you're obviously trying to watch your child while they sleep, and the camera doesn't have to take in a wide field of vision. Just try to position it closer to the crib or bed. High-tech and relatively cheap: You can purchase an additional (and much more powerful) IR lamp for around $40 online, and you can also probably find one in your local sporting goods or home security stores. Your child will still be sleeping in total darkness, but the MBP20's infrared camera will be able to see much, much more. This won't make the video screen on the parent unit any larger, but it should maximize what you can see. Good luck! ."}
