{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004XV11D2', 'answerTime': 'Apr 8, 2015', 'unixTime': 1428476400, 'question': 'Does it have a valve that essentially shrinks the size of the straw? Looking for a good thick smoothie straw cup, with a non-removable straw.', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I'm not sure if I understand the question completely... But the straw consists of 2 pieces: the long part inside the cup and part that it fits into which has a valve that keeps it from leaking and the top part comes out of the lid where the child drinks from. You can't remove the straw unless you take it apart. That being said, the straw is pretty flexible and I'm not sure if it could handle the viscosity of a smoothie."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B004XV11D2', 'answerTime': 'Feb 3, 2015', 'unixTime': 1422950400, 'question': 'Would this be good for a 7 month old learning to use a cup?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I don\'t remember what age I gave it to my son, but I think it is a good "first" cup. Mine didn\'t leak, so that\'s very helpful for containing a mess and making it more likely that you will let him play with it and use it more often. The straw top is closed and has slits that allow liquid to come through when your baby takes a sip; at the same time, your baby doesn\'t have to sip too hard to get some liquid out. It\'s got a narrow body that can feel like bottle (if you don\'t use the handles).'}
