{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Y99M52', 'answerTime': 'Nov 16, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416124800, 'question': 'what would be matching (that you can just put that car seat in and go) stroller for this car seat? ()', 'answer': "This car seat is not made to go into ANY stroller. It's not that type of seat. Generally, only infant carriers do that, not convertibles. I don't know of a single convertible car seat that is made stroller compatible."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B004Y99M52', 'answerTime': 'Nov 26, 2014', 'unixTime': 1416988800, 'question': 'I am an American living in Japan. Does this seat fit small cars? Can it be used on an airplane, and if so, is it viable (too big, too heavy)?', 'answer': 'This will be a tight squeeze if you are using it rear facing in a small vehicle, but forward facing it would fit fine. It is very heavy compared to a regular infant seat and I usually have to have my husband help me when I need to switch cars. It is very comfortable for my daughter, but I highly doubt it will fit on an airplane comfortably. A large international flight you would be ok, but on any smaller jets you would be hard pressed to make it fit. Hope that helps!'}
{'answer': "I think it really depends on the height proportions of the child. We haven't had his height measured lately, but we have an 11.5 month-old child who is about 23 lbs in size 4 diapers and wearing 18-month sized clothing. If I were to take a guess, he's probably in the 30-32 inch height range. He's still sitting comfortably in the rear-facing position with 4 to 5 inches to spare before his feet touch the backrest of the vehicle's bench seat. One thing to keep in mind is that growth in overall height doesn't necessarily translate proportionally between torso and and legs. Our baby's torso seems to be growing at the same pace as his legs (about evenly distributed in length), so even a 5 inch growing spurt might only translate to a 2 or 3 inch growth from the feet to the groin. Based upon his current growth rate, I'm suspecting we will keep our child in rear-facing mode until at least 18 months of age.", 'asin': 'B004Y99M52', 'answerTime': '5 days ago', 'question': 'What is the maximum child length this will accommodate with the seat rear facing?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
