{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005KWLEVW', 'answerTime': 'Sep 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380438000, 'question': 'Is it messy?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "It partially depends on what you put in it and how well you baby does keeping stuff in their mouth. Cut up fruit didn't seem to cause any issues. I tried some thick applesauce and although it started coming out of the holes, it wasn't too bad either."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005KWLEVW', 'answerTime': 'Aug 13, 2013', 'unixTime': 1376377200, 'question': 'Can this only be used with frozen foods? Can you use this with fresh food as well?', 'answer': "It can be used with fresh foods, that's primarily what I used it for, such as bananas, strawberries, etc. The size only fits about the amount of a large strawberry, so fresh fruit needs to be cut up before putting it in there. I started with just putting fresh apple sauce in it until my boys got good at sucking the fruit out. It does take some effort to get the fresh fruit out."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005KWLEVW', 'answerTime': 'Oct 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381734000, 'question': "What's it for (I know it's a feeder but how does it work?)", 'answer': "You put the food in the nipple part and the kid sucks on it like a pacifier. Bits of food get sucked out through the holes in the pacifier part. It works great & is dishwasher-friendly, but if you're using something juicy, or if your kid is slobbery, then when they turn it upside down, all the juice that collected in it when it's in the direction as pictured runs out the other end and onto your kid and your floor. The idea is to be able to give babies real fruit (or whatever) at an age where fruit would otherwise be a choking hazard."}
