{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005XB57Z4', 'answerTime': 'Apr 24, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398322800, 'question': 'Are the diapers covered by warranty at all?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The diapers are covered by warranty. Each diaper has it's own code with the tags, and you must register online for the warranty. I intended to, but never did. I have had only one issue, which was stictching at the tag in back had come loose on two (of 20), but I simply sewed it back up! I very strongly recommend these diapers!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005XB57Z4', 'answerTime': 'Jul 3, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404370800, 'question': 'How does this one-size compare to the Small or Medium size Fuzzy Bunz? If I get these to have more diapers, will they work as she grows?', 'answer': "I had a better experience with the specific sized Fuzzibunz (S,M, Lg) than I have had with these one size ones. The adjustable elastics inside seem to stretch quite a bit, as does the liner fabric. I had to use other diapers for a time because even on the smallest settings, they were leaking. My son was 9.5 lbs at birth, so it's not because I had a small baby. I also have found that I need to use a doubler with these when I use them, even when it's not for overnight (I don't even use these for overnight). When I used the ones that were specific sizes, I used them with one insert during the day and with a doubler at night without any problems at all, and was even able to use one set for two children. These newer one size ones don't seem like they'd hold up well enough to make it through another baby. My son is now 11 months old, is big and chubby, and the diapers have stretched out enough that he's still on the smallest settings. Not sure how much longer he'll last in them."}
