{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005XTCOBQ', 'answerTime': 'Feb 11, 2014', 'unixTime': 1392105600, 'question': 'Are these easy to remove? I want to be able to put them on at night and remove them during the day.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "These are VERY easy to remove. Just a simple squeeze where they connect will pop them apart. (As long as you aren't applying any glue to them). Both of my boys were able to pop them off around 2 years old, I use glue to the connection parts so my 2 1/2 year old can't open the doors. They DO get weak after awhile,(also why I glue mine) so if you are constantly taking them apart everyday, you MAY have to purchase extra packs to have on hand."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005XTCOBQ', 'answerTime': 'Nov 30, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385798400, 'question': 'Do you think these would work on the old 1930-40 door knobs that we have?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I'm not sure of the style of doorknib you're referring to, but this is essentially a plastic cage that fits around the doorknib, and you turn the knob by fitting your fingers through the holes pictured. On modern doorknibs, there is a little bit of play, so if they're even a little bit larger than today's standard, they should still fit (and work). Of course, if they're too snug, then a child could turn the knob just by grabbing the cover...but I THINK the doorknobs you're talking about are smaller than today's standard, so I think you'll be just fine."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005XTCOBQ', 'answerTime': 'Aug 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1407222000, 'question': 'How do you measure door knob to see if these fit?', 'answer': 'It fits our regular door knob (house built in 1999) with lots of room to spare. Unless you have an abnormally large door know it should be fine.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B005XTCOBQ', 'answerTime': 'Jul 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1404802800, 'question': 'Will they work to help protect the wall or another door when the door knob contacts them?', 'answer': 'No, there is no cushion to them and the part that would hit the wall has a raised circle ridge. For door to door contact there may be less damage if the door is a solid core. Paint may chip. For walls I would recommend door wall protectors(available on amazon for less than $3). They are plastic disks that adhere to the wall. There are hinged door stops(available on amazon for less than $3) as well. These only allow your door to swing 100 degrees instead past the frame.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B005XTCOBQ', 'answerTime': 'May 31, 2014', 'unixTime': 1401519600, 'question': 'Do these prevent the child from accessing the lock button on the door knob?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'Depends on the door, but I would think not.'}
