{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Oct 20, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382252400, 'question': 'Any recalls on this product?', 'answer': "I have not seen any recalls on this chair. However, their have been a couple of reviews with concerns. I went online and found that their were a few purchaser's that said the harness strap was not sufficient. I have not found that to be the case. My granddaughter has been using this chair at my house for about 2 months. I'm very pleased with the quality, and its sturdiness. As with any product, I do not let the baby be unattended, which I'm sure is the case with you."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Nov 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384070400, 'question': 'The twins that will use this chair are big at 7 mo. How long can I use it? weight or height limitations?', 'answer': 'The manufacturer specs say it should not be used for a child over 37 lbs. Our granddaughter is very big for her age, is now ten months old and doing fine in it. It secures very well to the table and when screwed in tight it\'s extremely solid (BTW, manufacturer states table should be solid, stable and from .8" to 3.3" thick). One quirk: Italians must loosen and tighten screws etc. the opposite of us. You turn the hook on chair arms to the left to tighten, right to loosen. This chair is very portable, easy to clean. Our own sons were raised in high chairs. This takes up less room and can easily be taken anywhere. I highly recommend this chair. Carol in SC'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Oct 11, 2013', 'unixTime': 1381474800, 'question': 'Would the arms damage a dining room table?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "No, I think not. I'm using ours on a very special cherry table my husband made but I usually put one of those large silicon baking mats underneath the arms. I use a silicon mat simply because I don't have a plastic placemat. If you use a place mat, get one that sort of 'hugs' the table and is not slippery. But truthfully, when you tighten the arms that chair is going absolutely nowhere. My daughter-in-law cuts up the baby's food and puts it right on the table. The mat makes it easier to clean up afterwards and also protects the table when the baby is banging her spoon and cup. This chair is great esp. if you don't have a lot of room for a high chair etc. Cleans easily, folds for travel and thanks to the harness, really secures the baby. I think it's terrific and I recommend it highly. C. Bane Charleston, SC"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Sep 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378450800, 'question': 'Does this chair work on a table with an apron? I have just 2.5" of table edge then a 3.5" tall apron.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'That can be a problem, because your babies legs need to slid under that apron. That said, our apron is 4 inches & our 7 month old granddaugther is still able to use this seat at our dining room table.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Apr 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396594800, 'question': 'Is liquirizia &gt; black?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Nov 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383462000, 'question': 'weight limit', 'answer': "The manufacturer recommendation is 37lbs. It's also mentioned in the product details section"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Mar 25, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395730800, 'question': 'Has anyone uses this for a baby who is not sitting up on their own yet? My son is 5 1/2 months and not quite there, but we need to use this soon.', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "I probably wouldn't use this on a baby that's not sitting on their own yet. You have to remember that this chair attaches to the table do if they fall forward, they'll hit the edge of the table."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006JV4HFS', 'answerTime': 'Nov 6, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383724800, 'question': 'What is the min. length from the edge of the table the rubber clamps underneath can go? I have only 2 inch of depth until a 5" height apron', 'answer': 'While I don\'t know what a 5" apron means, I can tell you the Inglesina chair works for us and we have about 2-2.5" of clearance under our table. The rubber clamp is about 1.5 inches wide. The room above the table is more of a concern. Having an obstruction under the table makes it difficult for us to aggressively tighten the clamp. It gets pretty tight though and we haven\'t had any problems.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006JV4HKI', 'answerTime': 'Jul 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1375081200, 'question': 'Would it be secure to hook to a Granite breakfast bar? It is approx. 2.5 inches thick and there is approx. 14 inches depth (or hang over)', 'answer': 'It would work, I currently have mine secured to my breakfast bar, I also can move it to my huge 6 inch thick table too! This thing is great and really looks comfortable it has a lot of padding.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006JV4HKI', 'answerTime': 'Jul 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1406012400, 'question': 'Would it be safe to hook on to a glass table of 10 mm, less than half an inch thick?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "The default 100% safe answer is NO that is not safe. Though the table can most likely handle the torque and weight of your kiddo in the seat, it changes the physics just a bit when you snug the handles down on the glass like a vice. Supposing you install it on glass, you then have compression pressure on the glass in addition to the weight and torque of kiddo in the seat. Our kid would sometimes bounce around in the seat a little bit and bang on the table which in your case would cause a lot more stress on the glass (which doesn't give any clues that it is about to break), so I'd suggest not putting this seat on a glass table. I *might* reconsider if the glass were 25mm+ thick and if it were a smaller table, but when talking about the safety of my kid, I'd just avoid the situation 100% especially when you consider what could go wrong..."}
