{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006SFUD2G', 'answerTime': 'Apr 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396422000, 'question': 'Do you think this travel bottle warmer would work with small 5 oz glass bottles? Thanks.', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes, we have also used it with 5 oz glass bottles (specifically Dr Browns). It's a little trickier because the warmer is larger than they are, so you have to wedge them into the warmer a little higher than the larger bottles but it absolutely works just as well in our experience."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006SFUD2G', 'answerTime': 'Mar 28, 2015', 'unixTime': 1427526000, 'question': 'Does this work with glass bottles??', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "it should work, it gets warm so the glass bottle will get warm as well. This is what I do because the bottle takes about 15 to 30 minutes to warm up. I have notes of about every how often my baby wants milk and I have noticed is every 2.5 hours to 3 hours. Since the bottle takes 30 minutes AND i have written down my baby's last and every meal. I know when he will be hungry so I start warming the bottle 30 minutes in advance and keep it warm until my baby wakes up. NOW, since I use formula, I DO NOT insert the formula until my baby is ready to eat because sometimes it is too hot and I need some space to put some cold water to make it warm. I always carry a bottle with cold water JUST in case i need to warm it up and it is too hot. God bless all babies. Amen"}
