{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006VIHL48', 'answerTime': 'May 1, 2014', 'unixTime': 1398927600, 'question': 'Can I warm the glass bottle with the silicone sleeves on?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'By "warming" it, do you mean heat it in the microwave or in hot water? I haven\'t needed to warm the bottles yet, but you could set the whole thing in hot water, although the water will obviously drip from the sleeve when using it, I would think. These are VERY well made glass bottles, and the sleeve is very well made and durable as well.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006VIHL48', 'answerTime': 'Sep 9, 2013', 'unixTime': 1378710000, 'question': 'With the sleeve on, is the bottle protected enough? If a child was to throw it on concrete, would it break?', 'answer': 'My daughter is 11-months and has dropped the bottle (while crawling, not the highchair), with the sleeve, on hardwoods and ceramic title and the bottle has survived. Throwing it on concrete might be a bit much, though. However, we picked up some of the shoelace-like bands, with the snaps, and I loop it threw the holes of the sleeve then attach it to the stroller and that has worked out well. Hope that helps!!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006VIJOPW', 'answerTime': 'Sep 24, 2013', 'unixTime': 1380006000, 'question': 'Are these glass bottles with the nipples? Which nipples it include?', 'answer': "The 9 oz bottles come with medium flow nipples. The 5 oz bottles come with slow flow nipples. If you're breastfeeding, start with the 5 oz bottles. Breastfed babies don't usually take more than 4 oz per serving anyways; they eat less per serving, but more frequently. I didn't know this, so now I have a pack of 9 oz bottles sitting unused."}
