{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006ZVV0NO', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2015', 'unixTime': 1424937600, 'question': 'Will this hurt the tint on my windows (because of how you adhere it)? And/or, if I have tint, do I really need this?', 'answer': "My personal recommendation, please, please, please, stay away from this horrendous product if you love your car. It will run your Windows, and yes if you have a good enough tint then you don't need this or any other shades for that matter. Also, using this product will necessitate am expensive visit to fix the damage done by is adhesive."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B006ZVV0NO', 'answerTime': 'Jun 28, 2013', 'unixTime': 1372402800, 'question': 'Is it a spring frame or is it a stick on?', 'answer': "The frame will certainly flex, but I wouldn't describe it as spring like. The mechanism for clinging to the glass is similar to a static charge. My application is for a heat/sun shield in an aircraft glass cockpit. As long as the shield and glass are kept clean, they work very well."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B006ZVV0NO', 'answerTime': 'Dec 22, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419235200, 'question': "In California, I know you can't put dark tinted glass on the driver side window. Can you stick these sun shades on the driver side window?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "We haven't tried but I can't see why not. They weren't large enough to cover the rear passenger windows therefore it might be perfect for the driver's side however it will be a pain when you need to roll the window down."}
