{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Dec 30, 2012', 'unixTime': 1356854400, 'question': 'parent unit power supply: it says the parent units are battery powered (with rechargeable batteries included), but is there also an option to plug into the wall?', 'answer': "The parent units have cords that can be plugged directly into the wall. It's important to keep the cords nearby, because battery life on these are not super long. I wouldn't take it off the cord for more than a couple hours at a time. Luckily, it will make a tone when the battery is low instead of simply turning off without you realizing it."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Jul 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1374476400, 'question': 'Can the two parent units be used to communicate with one another? (Like a walkie talkie so parents can talk without being heard on the baby unit?)', 'answer': 'Great IDEA for a feature, but as far as I\'m aware, the "baby unit" is always in the communication loop. However, as I think about this, it might be possible to lower the volume on the baby unit to the point it can\'t be heard in the nursery so the parents COULD talk on the other 2 units. It\'s late tonight, but I\'ll check this theory out for you sometime tomorrow & re-edit this answer so we\'ll know for sure. ***stay tuned*** ----------- Update 7/23/13: Checked for you this morning & unfortunately, the "baby" unit\'s LOWEST volume level is still audible. So, no, you can\'t communicate between the two "parent" units without the "baby" unit broadcasting the sound. Even though you can talk to one another, "baby" can always hear. Hope that helped you!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Mar 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395558000, 'question': 'Has anyone tried this across two separate buildings? My mother has dementia and lives in a cottage just 10 feet next to our house. She has fallen, forgot that she has a Lifeline, and just yells out to me (fortunately she got up ok that time). She keeps...', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'i can honestly and highly recomend this product i live 100+ feet from a disabled man that i take care of and it works very well it muffels the background noise and allows his voice to come thru clearly i love it and when i talk to him he can hear me it is pretty loud i recomend this vtech product'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Oct 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413356400, 'question': 'Does parent units have separate power adapters? So that I can set them plugged in, always on, without having to worry about charging every night?', 'answer': 'Yes!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Feb 28, 2013', 'unixTime': 1362038400, 'question': 'Will two of these units conflict with the same frequency?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "We have two of these sets, one in our toddlers room and another in the baby's room right next door. They work without interference, however we have noticed our original set has started occasionally losing connection with the base unit a few times a week (not a big deal but the warning beep is annoying if it happens in the middle of the night). I'm not sure if it is directly related to adding a new base unit or just because the first set is over a year old. They always reconnect on their own in a few minutes though and we never have any problems with static."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B007NG5UEU', 'answerTime': 'Jan 13, 2015', 'unixTime': 1421136000, 'question': 'Do battery packs need to be put in the parent units or can they be plugged into an outlet w/o and will work provided kept in power adapter all times', 'answer': 'It plugs into an outlet and is powered this way. When I remove it from the plug, it stays charged for several hours.'}
