{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B008CMQTZI', 'answerTime': 'Feb 26, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393401600, 'question': 'We have 10month old active twins! Would this be suitable?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'It is and for two the extensions will be more room. I like mine the only problem I have with this play yard is when my 9 month old started pulling up on the sides sometimes she would slip and her arm would get stuck in between the slats. She never really got hurt but, but I was worried she was going to twist it the wrong way.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B008CMQTZI', 'answerTime': 'Feb 2, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391328000, 'question': 'Can i get a pad for the floor', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I bought the interlocking 24" foam colored blocks in Home Depot. Perfect!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008CMQTZI', 'answerTime': 'Sep 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1379401200, 'question': 'Hello, I have an almost 10 month old. Im wondering if I need to buy the extension panels with this, or is it big enough without? Thanks!', 'answer': 'Prior to purchasing this I had read some reviews that stated that the enclosure was on the small side. Not much larger than a standard-sized play-pen which we already had. One reviewer noted that it was almost more cost effective to just buy two playzones if you were thinking of buying the extension panels. It has been awhile since I did the math on that but we wound up taking that advice and buying two and connecting them in our living room. I am looking at the setup now and it makes about an 8\' x 5.5\' oval. We threw a 2" thick Alzip mat down inside (no detectable toxic out-gassing from the foam) for cushioning and set up all of our little one\'s toys in there. It has worked out pretty well. It\'s large enough for mom, grandma, or me to play with her in there and she has learned to crawl, stand upright, and has even started walking, all within the 2 playzones connected together. It\'s nice because it is a large enough enclosure to allow her to explore but keeps her away from glass, marble, and other furniture made from toddler unfriendly materials. She is almost one year old now and we would not have gotten anywhere close to that much use out of just one playzone. Plus I have the flexibility to break these down into smaller enclosures for travel, placing in multiple rooms, gating off other rooms, etc.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008CMQTZI', 'answerTime': 'Jun 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1371452400, 'question': '1. How easy is it to move the play zone from room to room or to outside? 2. How sturdy is it when baby tries to use the wall for support to stand up?', 'answer': "It would need to be disassembled before moving. You might be able just take apart a couple pieces and collapse, but it isn't sturdy enough to just move! Baby on the walls has been fine. My 11 month old does her darnedest to get out, but has never been successful. It never looks like its going to fall."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B008CMQTZI', 'answerTime': 'Nov 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384675200, 'question': "Can this support a baby's weight if she uses it to pull up/cruise?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes, our little boy pulls himself up all the time using it to help steady him. He and his friends have cruised around the edges holding on for balance (we have 2 fastened together so it's quite a large space)."}
