{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B008O9PYFK', 'answerTime': 'Mar 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1395903600, 'question': 'Can it be used for a newborn? If not, why?', 'answer': "I would say its risky. I personally wouldn't want anything around my newborn. I would be worried about their neck getting in a position where they can't breathe. I got it when my son was 4 months old and he immediately came off of it and put it over his face (to chew on). I ended up attaching it to the sheets/mattress pad with safety pins. In a month and a half he figured out how to get his arm under it, which made the some of the pins come out. Glad I have a monitor because he could have poked himself. Anyway I quit using it. Not really worth it for a month and a half use plus I didn't see any improvement in his flat head."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B008O9PYFK', 'answerTime': 'Jul 21, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405926000, 'question': 'We are trying to prevent my sons flat head from getting worse! Is this something that can be used in the crib to help? He is 4 months old.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "This can be used anywhere. We used it for my son when he naps in our bed, sleep through the night in his crib, or laying on the sofa while watching tv. Whenever my son is laying on his back or side, we use this pillow every time. Also, I would not hesitate to get this pillow. I took before and after photos of the back of my son's head, and after a month, you really notice the difference."}
