{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': 'Nov 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1415779200, 'question': 'Can you heat up the pouches for foods with meat in them? i.e. Chicken &amp; butternut squash', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'The way I do it is I put pouch In a bowl with warm to hot water for like 4-5 minutes and it heats it up. I do double check the temperature of the pure before giving in to my baby. You can definitely do it with any meat and veggies..'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': 'Mar 18, 2015', 'unixTime': 1426662000, 'question': 'Can anyone share their experience using these with yogurt and any yogurt based recipes? Do yogurt based pouches freeze &amp; thaw well?', 'answer': 'I use these all the time for yogurt and they work great! You would have to make sure they are fully unthawed before eating unlike go gurts those since the spout is smaller'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': 'Dec 29, 2013', 'unixTime': 1388304000, 'question': 'Do you have problems with these clogging if the food has texture or small chunks like quinoa or steel cut oats?', 'answer': "I had some luck with textures- but also some trouble. It really depends. Most everything went in fine, but not always out easily. My oatmeal was fine- but it was also pured with fruit, which helps. I had a ground turkey/peas/carrots combo that gave me trouble. In the end, the clogs weren't bad thru the spout, but much more difficult while using the spoon attachment. I loved the system so much for storage, that in the end, if there was a major clog, I'd just cut off the top and spoon feed from the pouch."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': '3 days ago', 'question': 'Do I need to cook the fruit?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Most ripe fruit will pure just fine without having to cook it. The more dense fruits and veg you should roast off In the oven or steam them till soft.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': 'Feb 27, 2014', 'unixTime': 1393488000, 'question': 'What kind of foods have you put in the pouches that can be easily sucked out? Sweet potato was too thick for my LO to suck out.', 'answer': "Don't put sweet potato by itself...mix it down with apple or pear. I usually use apple or pear sauce as a base to whatever else I mix with it. Apple, sweet potato & carrot with a small amount of maple syrup has been a favorite. When I make apple sauce I tend to have an overabundance of granny smith apples so I add a little maple syrup to offset the tart flavor of the apple. Look up online any of the well known name brand pouches and see what kind of mixes they are putting together. That is where I got a lot of ideas. For instance, pear, blueberry & beet. I am mixing the nutritive veggies/fruits a lot heavier than you see in the commercial pouches, but again if you use apple or pear sauce as a base it will be naturally sweeter and a little thinner than most things you would put in by themselves."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B009IWNPXK', 'answerTime': 'Oct 12, 2014', 'unixTime': 1413097200, 'question': 'Are these sterilized and ready to go or do you need to clean them before using?', 'answer': 'Ready for use!'}
