{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00A0ID6YO', 'answerTime': 'Oct 31, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383202800, 'question': 'Hello, I would like to know the voltage input of the product; if it can take 220 V for South America or it only stands 110 V. Thanks!!', 'answer': "No where in the manual does it mention anything about voltage, neither 110 or 220 v. But of course it works perfectly on 110 v here in the U.S. Neither is there any indication of a voltage selection switch on the equipment (both the camera and the monitor need to be plugged in -- neither works on just batteries). So I would assume it's safe to advise you that, at least for the products sold in the U.S., they will only work on 110 v. If you need to work them on 220 v, you might be able to use a voltage adapter (available in many stores), but there is also (besides the question of voltage) the question of Hz, or alternating current frequency. I know that some places in the world operate on 50 Hz rather than the U.S.'s 60 Hz, and this might also be a problem -- perhaps also handled by the adapter, but perhaps not."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00A0ID6YO', 'answerTime': 'Mar 10, 2014', 'unixTime': 1394434800, 'question': 'Can this system monitor sound from 2 different rooms at the same time if you have 2 cameras? Or is it just to be used for different angles in one room?', 'answer': 'Probably the company can answer that better. 1-800-268-6237. But it appears that it cannot monitor from 2 different rooms, but from whatever angle you have it pointed at in one room. It doesn\'t have extra "ports", though there may be a gadget that could make that possible. However I\'m not sure you can buy a second camera separately to be used with one monitor. You may need a more sophisticated system, which would could cost much more.'}
