{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Mar 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1362297600, 'question': 'Does Britax Advocate 70-G3 Convertible Car Seat fit in small sedans, like Elantra?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "All I can is that it fits in our '08 Honda Civic Sedan (which is considered a compact car) just fine. :)"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Feb 21, 2013', 'unixTime': 1361433600, 'question': 'Is the Britax Advocate 70-G3 Convertible Car Seat Seat suitable for new born baby?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': 'Yes, this seat may be used in the rear-facing position for a newborn baby that is over 5 lbs. Infant Positioning Insert may be necessary for small infants to achieve a snug fit of the harness around your child (sold separately). However, you may find an "infant carseat" more satisfactory and/or convenient for the first six months of your baby\'s life, as they are portable. We moved up to the Britax Advocate 70-G3 when our baby out-grew her "infant seat". Furthermore, if expense is key and you can not aford to buy two carseats, infant seat and convertable seat, then get the convertable seat as it will last you the longest and it is the best value for your money. Good luck with your little one!'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Jan 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388822400, 'question': 'which one is better for 70-G3 baby?Britax Marathon or Advocate?', 'answer': "By the price Advocate seems to be a more advanced model. However, if your car's back seat space is limited, and you frequently need to sit 2 alduts in the back with the baby, then I would suggest you go with the Marathon."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Apr 14, 2013', 'unixTime': 1365922800, 'question': 'Has Britax yet eliminated hazardous flame retardants from its car seats? The company had promised to do so by the end of 2012.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'After searching and searching for myself I found this at http://www.britaxusa.com/learning-center/safety-standards .... "flammability standard referenced above, FMVSS 302, is intended to prevent harm to occupants in the event of post-accident vehicle fires. BRITAX and its suppliers are currently compliant to all applicable federal and state standards regarding chemical compliance as well as more internal stringent standards. In 2011, BRITAX announced that we would be revising our internal chemical compliance specifications. These revised specifications require all BRITAX suppliers to reduce and/or where possible, eliminate the use of all chemicals containing bromine or chlorine from all components used in our car seats and all other products - while still ensuring their ability to pass federal government standards for flammability. We set a target for our suppliers to be compliant to this new standard by the end of 2012. We are pleased to report successful progress. Our suppliers have worked very diligently toward meeting these more stringent chemical compliance specifications and have successfully reduced and/or eliminated the use of all chemicals containing bromine and chlorine in all components as of December 31, 2012. BRITAX remains committed to working closely with our suppliers to ensure continued leadership in the area of chemical compliance specifications. In BRITAX'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Nov 17, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384675200, 'question': 'Can you adjust the recliner on the fly? For example, can I reach back and recline the seat after my kid fall asleep?', 'answer': "No you cannot adjust the seat while driving. There is a saftey mechanism in the seat. Once it's clipped in the seat will not recline. You have to set the position in advance."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00A2HUF9M', 'answerTime': 'Aug 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1377154800, 'question': 'Can the children undo the chest clip?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I think it woukd be awfully diffucult for a child to undo. We've had ours for several months & the latch is slightly more worn in (thus easier to undo) but there's no way my 2 yr old could pull it apart unless i didnt snap it in all the way."}
