{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00AHPZY68', 'answerTime': 'Jan 4, 2014', 'unixTime': 1388822400, 'question': "I have an older car that doesn't have the hooks for the latch system. Can you buckle this car seat in with the seatbelt?", 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes, I just tested it out at store and I don't have Latch either. It's a pain to initially install with the seatbelt, but seems it will stay put much better than our current base for our infant carseat. I tested a Britax as well and it was the same issue, so no difference on that based on brand."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00AHPZY68', 'answerTime': 'Dec 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386144000, 'question': 'Can all of the recline angles be used in both front and rear facing? Can the angle be adjusted AFTER it is latched or belted into the seat?', 'answer': "No, you can't use all the recline angles. For rear facing I was able to use only 1, 2 and 3. For front facing I can only use #7. If I try to use other angles the level mark gets out of the right angle. I have a Honda oddysey, maybe in a different vehicle you might be able to use different angles, but I doubt. About your second question yes, you can make the adjustments but the problem I found is that the handle is placed in the front bottom which in rear facing position gets too close to the back of the car seat so it gets a little tricky to put your hand there, it's really tight. I hope my answer helps."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00AHPZY68', 'answerTime': 'Apr 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396940400, 'question': 'How does this compare to the radian rxt? Has anyone used them both?', 'answer': "The Radian allows for a higher weight limit RF (although most kids will not reach it). The NextFit has a lower RF weight limit, but a higher RF height limit than the Radian. NextFit is the 2nd tallest in RF height (after the Graco Headwise/Size4Me/MySize/Fit4Me). So, if your child is very tall but not super heavy, go for the NextFit (or one of the aforementioned Graco seats). If your child is chunky but not that tall go for the radian. Regardless, both seats will see your child RF to about 4yo (NHTSA recommendation is rear face to at least age 2, and as close to age 4 as possible). The Radian has lower sides, the NextFit has a no-rethread harness. NextFit has an easier LATCH install. Radian has a booster seat option, but it's almost useless since it is outgrown for most children at the same time as the forward facing harness. Both Radian (with the optional angle adjustor) and NextFit tend to fit well behind tall people or in smaller cars. ETA: NextFit harness is a little trickier to adjust around the child - especially in seats manufactured before Oct 2013. Check out Car Seats for the Littles on Facebook or on their blog for more car seat info and reviews of both seats. (I'm not affiliated with them, just a fan)"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00AHPZY68', 'answerTime': 'Oct 24, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414134000, 'question': 'What is the rear facing seated height limit?', 'answer': "There isn't one for this seat as the height extender goes to full size before booster rear facing as well as front. Our state is rear facing until 1yr but pedio and hospital are recomending two years. Our son is very long (off the growth chart for 13 months ) and fist rear facing just fine."}
