{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00AHVR7ZI', 'answerTime': 'Dec 3, 2013', 'unixTime': 1386057600, 'question': 'Does the childs head fall foward when sleeping', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "One of the best things about this seat is it's ability to have a bit of a recline. If you are able to make the rear seat recline a little (just enough so the head doesn't fall forward) the seat has an easy loosen feature just for this :) You still want them to be sitting up enough that the shoulder belt is still in the right place and useful (if needed). But they can be back a little bit and use the headrests in the booster as pillows. Hope this helps, we have had this booster for over a year now and love it :)"}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00AHVR7ZI', 'answerTime': 'Oct 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1382425200, 'question': 'Does it fit in a standard airline seat? It looks like it might be a bit wide (16.5 in. vs. recommended 16 in.). Thanks.', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': "I don't know the answer to that questions because we do not use booster seats on airplanes. It is my understanding that booster seats are not approved for airline use because they are designed to work with shoulder straps, which are not present on airplanes. Added height is therefore not necessary,as only a lap belt is in use. We typically check our boosters (at no additional cost) and the kids are strapped in the seat belt like an adult."}
