{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00BMJPEFY', 'answerTime': 'Sep 23, 2013', 'unixTime': 1379919600, 'question': 'Does the cameras picture shut off after 4-5 minutes (like a screen saver feature)? If so is this feature able to be adjusted or turned off?', 'answer': 'The picture remains on, it has never shut off like a screen saver, and it is a very clear picture, even at night. hope this helps. You should also know that there are many advanced features, so you wish, you can set it to keep the picture off until there is noise, and you pick the level of noise that will trigger the picture. It really is a good monitor if you can get it at a good price.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00BMJPEFY', 'answerTime': 'Nov 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385107200, 'question': 'Do cordless phones (landline--not cell) interfere with this system?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'The monitor & receiver do operate at 2.4 GHz, which is common for cordless phones & wi-fi. Check your cordless phone to see what GHz it operates at, that could give you a good indication.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00BMJPEFY', 'answerTime': 'Nov 22, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385107200, 'question': 'What is the working range from monitor to the camera?', 'answer': "We have used the camera for 3 months for naps and nighttime. We have an old lathe-and-plaster house, so the walls are much denser than a newer house. The baby's camera is in the far corner of the house and we can get reception 4 rooms away (about 40 feet) but no further. There is a beep when you lose reception so you always know without looking if it's working. The furthest I've gone is 150 feet outside and it's been fine. Unless the whole house is between me and the camera."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00BMJPEFY', 'answerTime': 'Nov 4, 2013', 'unixTime': 1383552000, 'question': 'Does this monitor "pan" as well as zoom?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "Yes, sort of. It pans when zoomed in. The actual camera doesn't move, but the zoomed in portion can be panned within the original area of view."}
