{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00BWIPD42', 'answerTime': 'Jul 12, 2013', 'unixTime': 1373612400, 'question': 'Is there a way to anchor it to the ground or sand? Could a child treat it like a giant hampster cage?', 'answer': 'a) There\'s no manufacturer provided way to anchor it down. There is, however, four elastic loops on the bottom of each "corner" that you may be able to use to secure it down with some of your own rope, etc. b) I hate to say this, but I think it\'s a question of intent. If you have a heavy sleeper who might press against the sides of the tent while they\'re sleeping I don\'t think it\'s going to tip over as long as you have it on a flat surface (the ground). Some guy tried to use the older model on top of a bed and said it DID tip over. It makes sense since the bed would dip down where the kid was lying and the tent wouldn\'t have a flat surface to support itself to counteract the weight. My kids weigh about 35 pounds and could not get it to tip over just by rolling up against the sides with the tent on the floor. (She slept with her rear end and half her torso hanging out the tent opening and it did not fall over.) However, if you have a kid who\'s going to actively try to turn it into a hamster ball by throwing themselves around inside then it very well may tip over. Sorry if this isn\'t a lot of help.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00BWIPD42', 'answerTime': 'Jun 23, 2014', 'unixTime': 1403506800, 'question': 'Can a large active 3 month old use this.', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'I think so. The padding is below the base and I cannot see any suffocation hazards. The child will have a ton of space, but they will grow into it. We use it with our almost 2 year old and started using it with her at 18 months old. I prefer over a pack and play. And it is so small its easy to take on trips!'}
