{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00D04R3LW', 'answerTime': 'Feb 5, 2014', 'unixTime': 1391587200, 'question': 'How far can the crotch strap get from the child? Many seats force the strap too close (Graco Nautalis, for one) is it comparable to the Frontier?', 'answer': 'We have both the Frontier & the Nautilus. Without going into specific inches, i can tell you that the crotch strap slots are closer to the front of the seat (i.e., less "shallow") than on the Nautilus and it is also slightly longer and the button is easier to press to release (its still childproof, but i feel like I am not going to break my thumb in the process). The way the lap hooks are positioned also allow for more room so they\'re not clamping down on my sons inner thighs. Hands down the configuration is much better than the Nautilus. The price does reflect that, but trust me, it\'s worth it to be frustration-free!'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00D04R3LW', 'answerTime': 'Nov 10, 2013', 'unixTime': 1384070400, 'question': "My son is using my daughter's old Frontier. I want something newer. Would the Pioneer 70 be a good option to replace my old Original 2009 Frontier?", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "I think it would be a good choice. They are very similar. The biggest thing to consider would be whether or not your son likes a reclining seat. The Pioneer can be installed reclined, but it doesn't recline very much. This is perfect for my son, because he likes to be sitting up, and he feels a bit smushed when the boosters/car seats recline at all, so I put it in straight up. Even straight up, though, his head stays back when he falls asleep. The harness is great and keeps him securely in, and he sleeps better in this on long car rides than he ever did in his other seats. If your son likes a deeper recline, you might be better to go with a Frontier. For the money, though, I think the Pioneer is great. Hope this helps, and good luck!"}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00D04R3LW', 'answerTime': 'Apr 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1397458800, 'question': 'How easy is this seat to install? We are looking for a five point harness that will be transferred to different cars and installed by other parents.', 'answer': "We have two Pioneer 70s in my car and two Frontier 90s each in my wife's car and our nanny's car. I have to remove the two 90s from our nanny's car each Friday and reinstall them each Monday morning so I have a lot of experience with the installation of that seat. The differences between installation of the 70s and 90s is minimal, however. I utilize the latch system for install so I don't have any insight on how hard or easy it is to install either seat using the lap belt. However, neither seat is as difficult to install using latch as some reviews suggest once you learn a little trick that isn't discussed in the owner's manual. Unlike some of the Britax baby seats, both the 90 and the 70 utilize a one button, single belt system to which both latch connectors are attached. So the buckle is on the left side only and you don't have a buckle on each side as with some Britax baby seats. Wth that in mind, the intallation process goes like this. After loosening the left side buckle and connecting both latch connectors to the latches on the car seat frame you are left with about 8 to 12 inches of belt slack above the buckle. That portion of the belt has a loop at the top. What you need to do is look for a small cutaway in the fabric on the left side (buckle side) of the booster seat. The cutaway is located right where the backrest and deck of the booster meet up. Essentially where your child's left hip would be if he or she was sitting in the seat. Slip the looped portion of the belt through that cutaway such that the slack in the belt is on the inside of the left armrest. Take a rigid tool such as a crescent wrench or a screwdriver and slip it through the loop. Either sit in the seat or place your body weight on the seat using your knee so as to push the booster seat back against the vehicle seat. While applying pressure to the booseter seat pull the looped portion of the belt straight up toward the headliner and slightly to the right of the seat to tighten the booster to the vehicle seat. When you're done just tuck the looped portion back through the cutaway in the fabric so your child doesn't have to sit on it. Using the rigid tool to tighten the seat makes things easier on your hands but isn't essential. You can do it bare handed. However, if you don't get the looped portion of the belt to the inside of the armrest by slipping it through the cutaway, I've found that you will never get the angle necessary to tighten the booster seat to the vehicle seat to your satisfaction. I'm a bit of a fanatic about making sure that my kids seats are tight, tight tight to the vehicle seat and could never get either seat installed without unacceptable wiggle if I installed them as instructed in the owners manual. If you install either the 70 or the 90 using the method above, you can get both seats in in about 5 minutes. I had the choice of using the 90s or the 70s in our nanny's car and choose the 90s for two reasons. First, the 90s just feel sturdier. My thought was that if I was going to put the seats through heavy duty use by taking them in and out each week, the 90s might hold up better. I don't know if that's true but it was my impression. The second reason is that the nylon used in the 90s appears to be a bit thicker than what was used in the 70s. One of the biggest issues that crops up when seats are taken in and out frequently is that of the nylon belt tangling in the buckle. It can be a huge pain to untangle a belt that's turned in the buckle and I've found to my chagrin that it's even possible for the nylon to invert (which causes one of the two buckles to become upside down) if the tangle is severe enough. I thought I might avoid that problem by going with the 90s. On the other hand the 70s are lighter which can be an advantage depending on who is going to be taking the seats out and putting them back in. Bottom line, both seats are good quality seats and are fairly easy to remove and install if you use the method described above. The 90s feel noticably sturdier and may last longer if the seats are going to come in and out frequently. The 90s are more expensive though. The 70s are lighter than the 90s and that could be an advantage too. Good luck!"}
