{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00D3EWJQI', 'answerTime': 'Nov 26, 2013', 'unixTime': 1385452800, 'question': 'can you connect this camera to a tablet or tv also your computer if so hows the quality', 'answer': "Yes, you can connect this camera's monitor to both a tablet and to a TV, if you want. I've not personally done the TV connection but I do view the camera feed on my Android phone and on my tablet. For tablet viewing, Lorex provides an application available from the Play Store called Lorex Care. This allows the user to connect to the feed remotely ONLY when the monitor is cradled in the Wifi dock; which is an additional purchase unless it comes with your package. Note that that is the only way to get a feed wirelessly anywhere else than on the monitor itself. The Lorex Care app is okay and allows me access to three cameras I have in my home. I can hear audio and choose to manually rotate between cameras. On the monitor (not the wifi cradle) there is a 3.5mm A/V out port. There is an additional add-on that you can buy from Lorex which is a 3.5mm to RCA adapter that will allow connection to your TV from your monitor. With respect to connecting it and viewing on your computer, I don't believe that there is a Window O/S or Mac version of Lorex Care, but I really haven't looked either."}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00D3EWJQI', 'answerTime': 'Apr 8, 2014', 'unixTime': 1396940400, 'question': 'When more than one camera is connected, can you hear sound from both cameras simultaneously?', 'answerType': 'N', 'answer': 'No, you can\'t hear sound from more than one camera simultaneously. Depending on which monitor you have (I have the BB4325X), the problem is solved through the monitor\'s "scan" function, which cycles through all your available camera views for about five seconds at a time. For whatever view is on the monitor, the sound (or lack thereof) is what you would hear. The only exception would be for some models which present "quad" view which presents up to four views at once. For this quad view, no sound is offered. I believe that the reason this is is that 1.) it may be difficult to have all that consolidated audio streaming, and 2.) you really wouldn\'t know where the sound is coming from. The scan feature is what we use across our three cameras for our 3 year old and set of twins. Works great. Also, even on screen saver (where the screen goes dark), the audio cycling still happens.'}
