{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00FZGLO1S', 'answerTime': 'Dec 30, 2014', 'unixTime': 1419926400, 'question': 'after you take it out the microwave, do you just let it sit on the kitchen counter to dry off? and if so, how long on average does it take to dry?', 'answer': 'I usually dry it off at the bottle dryer rack. If you just leave it as is on the counter, will probably take about 30-35 minutes.'}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00FZGLO1S', 'answerTime': 'Oct 26, 2014', 'unixTime': 1414306800, 'question': 'Do you still have to wash bottles and nipples first with soap, before sterilizing? Or can you put them in there right after baby uses them?', 'answer': "You still have to wash everything with soap before sterilizing, since the steam can't remove crusted on milk, etc. BUT the design is clever, because the inner box that holds all the little pieces can be put in the dishwasher, and then you can either consider those pieces clean AND sterile, or you can quickly steam it after so it's been separately washed and sterilized. What I did was keep the whole microwave box on the counter, near my normal dish dying rack, so that I could wash each item in the sink as I used it and then place it directly in the steamer. Then, at the end of the day, or whenever I needed those items, I'd sterilize everything in the steamer, and let it all dry with the lid off for a little while. That system worked pretty well, even though we have little counter space, it was worth it to keep the steamer right there all the time."}
