{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00GTX269A', 'answerTime': 'Sep 15, 2014', 'unixTime': 1410764400, 'question': 'My baby usually ends up rolling into a corner of her crib in the night. Will this create false alarms?', 'answerType': 'Y', 'answer': "I haven't had ANY false alarm. I LOVE this! It was actually used in a research study!! Well worth the money. I LOVE that you can roll over and see it blinking to have peace of mind. As a NICU mom that came home with babies on medical monitors.. I feel this is just as good. Be careful of the sensitivity though. It can pick up fans and if the bassinet is close to your bed it can pick up your movements. So be sure to test it without the baby in while the fan is going and other regular movements around it. :-)."}
{'questionType': 'open-ended', 'asin': 'B00GTX269A', 'answerTime': 'Jul 14, 2014', 'unixTime': 1405321200, 'question': "I'm really torn between this monitor and the angel care. Any experience with both?", 'answer': "I've only used the Babysense, but we have friends with the Angel Care who seem to get many more false alarms than we do. I was bothered by the flashing blue light when in operation, but our child seems unaffected by it."}
{'answer': 'We have the same style metal mesh and the sensors kept going off (false alarm). We contacted Hisense and they told use to cut a peice of plywood the same size as the mattress and place that on the springs then the sensors. Also we discovered that something as simple as a ceiling fan beating air on the mattress was making the monitor think our daughter was breathing. We discovered that while testing it without her in the crib. Definitely test everything without the baby in the crib. Hope this helped.', 'asin': 'B00GTX269A', 'answerTime': '26 days ago', 'question': 'We have a crib with the metal wire bottom, do we need to put something down to make it a flat surface or can the sensors be placed on that alone?', 'questionType': 'open-ended'}
