{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00ISS2KVI', 'answerTime': 'Sep 19, 2014', 'unixTime': 1411110000, 'question': 'Does this seat work like a 3-in-1, where it can become a booster for the older child?', 'answerType': '?', 'answer': 'It only works rear-facing and forward-facing with a 5 point harness. It is not a belt positioning booster, but it can last an older child for quite a while... up to 65lbs and 54in. According to the CDC growth charts, the average 7 year old (50th percentile) is about 50lbs and 48in.'}
{'questionType': 'yes/no', 'asin': 'B00ISS2KVI', 'answerTime': 'Aug 24, 2014', 'unixTime': 1408863600, 'question': "Do you think this is suitable for newborn babies? It looks big and I'm concerned about lack of support for baby and lack of recline.", 'answerType': '?', 'answer': "If you choose to skip the infant carrier (perfectly safe to do so) this seat is one that DOES fit newborns well. The strap slots are low enough to fit average and some smaller newborns well. This seat only has one recline line so it is important to make sure it is properly reclined (line level to the ground). There is some infant padding with the seat and rolled receiving blankets can be placed on either side of an infant to provide additional support and prevent any slouching over until baby has better head control. There are many Car Seat Safety Groups that recommend this seat as a choice for newborns who aren't using infant carriers."}
